WEBVTT

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:39.260
Studio Rikka

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:57.030
In the future, probably Japan.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:57.030
“Robots” have seen practical use for some time.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:57.030
This is an era just after the widespread deployment of “Androids.”

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.130
We’ve identified the information source.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:11.210
As expected, it’s from the same district.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.760
This time it was transmitted via more than five other androids.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.260
Somehow, the pattern diverged.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.560
Just as we tho—

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.560
Trace the recipient unit.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.690
Recipient identified.

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.060
I’ve found it on my end as well.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:32.780
I’m surprised.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.610
That is Shiotsuki’s parting gift, isn’t it?

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:36.820
What are you trying to say?

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:40.950
Shiotsuki’s ai, Code:life—do you recall that implementation experiment?

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:44.290
The one that you forcefully halted?

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.540
I didn’t know it had been continued.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:47.830
And externally, too.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:51.090
All related personnel are under protective observation.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.300
It’s all connected in ways I had not foreseen.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.710
In any case,

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:59.390
it’s almost certain that the androids in this district are receiving messages from that café.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.430
And all via communication among their own kind.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.930
Is this your doing as well, Shiotsuki?

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:09.270
Now tracing the location of the unit in question.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:16.360
<b>Time of EVE</b>

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.480
The log has been successfully written, Master.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:52.310
Question.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.110
What is it?

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:00.200
Well …

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.570
Cancel question.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:04.200
Coffee.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.080
Acknowledged.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:14.840
Where is—I mean,

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:16.920
Status: Family.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.050
Your father has taken up residence at his workplace.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:24.010
Your mother left before my activation.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.600
I believe Naoko-sama is still in bed.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.180
Time of return.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:29.930
3:18 a.m.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.060
I feel terrible.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.350
Then you shouldn’t have gone drinking.

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:35.520
It felt good at first.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.530
Order your own coffee!

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.950
After the party, this thing came to pick me up.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:45.950
I was so embarrassed.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.330
What about breakfast?

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:51.580
Cancel breakfast.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:52.770
I am really sorry.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.630
Could you please repeat—

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.630
One serving of breakfast immediately.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:55.630
Acknowledged.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:01.220
Our domestic food self-sufficiency ratio will soon exceed 80%.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.460
<b>Domestic food self-sufficiency ratio exceeds 80%</b>

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.220
<b>Domestic food self-sufficiency ratio exceeds 80%</b>

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.470
<b>Agricultural Ministry</b>

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.470
<b>The biggest pace of expansion since robot
labor was introduced into primary industries.</b>

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.470
This is the finding from an investigation by the Agricultural Ministry—

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.470
<b>Would you eat a tomato created by machines?</b>

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.980
<b>In such an age, treasure warmth.</b>

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.480
<b>Ethics Committee</b>

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:22.030
Whoa! In other words, “dori-kei” refers to a psychological dependence on androids, yes?

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:26.490
Recently, this is what young people who treat house-roids as humans are being called.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:29.990
We bring you our investigation of these “dori-kei” people.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.250
<i>Ah, how gross! They should just like … clear off.</i>

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.500
<i>They were all arm-in-arm and stuff.</i>

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.500
<i>Whoa, that’s intense!</i>

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.420
<i>Isn’t it just loneliness? Like if they don’t have any friends …</i>

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:43.760
You should be careful too.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.510
What do you mean by that?

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.180
<i>… Just order it around.</i>

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.930
Rikuo. I saw your sister yesterday.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:34.890
Midnight, right?

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:36.770
At the station turnstiles. She was pretty drunk.

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:37.810
She was properly escorted.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:38.770
You went?

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:39.770
Our android did.

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:42.440
Like I said …

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:46.450
You trust robots way too much.

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:48.450
It’s not really a matter of trust.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:49.950
Come on. The whatever laws.{he’s implying he knows the existence of the laws but doesn’t remember what they are, so i changed it to a statement-ish line instead of a question --rop}

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:51.330
The three laws of robotics.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:52.200
First Law.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.830
Do not inflict injury on humans. Protect humans.

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:57.120
Oh. Is that why it went to pick her up?

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.830
It’s not like they worry about humans.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:00.880
You have an android;
at least think about these things.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.840
You’re one to talk. You don’t even have one.

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:06.590
Hey, what do you think about this?

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:07.720
It’s a bunch of numbers.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:09.720
These are movement records for my android.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:10.470
Which part?

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.020
Are you free after this?

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.730
I want you to come with me.

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:20.350
Where to?

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:22.310
That’s … I don’t know.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:23.690
What’s up with that?

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:27.570
It has been acting strange recently, coming home at weird hours.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:28.820
Your robot?

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.490
So I got concerned and looked into its movement records.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:33.080
And …?

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:37.250
Twenty hours ago; that’d be yesterday afternoon?

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:38.790
It went off on its own.

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:42.330
That’s because … it had things to do, right?

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:47.970
Here?

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:58.890
<b>You’re lost</b>

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:57.470
Here? Seriously?

00:06:57.470 --> 00:06:58.890
I wonder if the gps’s calibration is off?

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:00.980
This isn’t a place that robots would come to.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:19.410
Hey.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:32.380
Doesn’t it seem suspicious?

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:33.930
If we don’t go in …

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:40.390
It <i>is</i> kinda suspicious.

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:41.440
Then should we go home?

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.560
Time of Eve’s <b>Rule</b>

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.560
<b>In this café,</b>

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.560
there is to be no discrimination

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.560
<b>between humans and robots.</b>

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.560
Customers, please cooperate

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.560
Obey the rule and have a fun time... Nagi

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:07.000
This is—

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:08.050
Welcome!

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:10.710
Please take any seat that’s free.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.090
I’ll take your orders soon.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:15.550
Right. Over there, yeah?

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:18.180
It might only be a short while,

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:20.770
but enjoy yourselves, all right?

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:34.490
A café?

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:34.490
It’s a café.

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:35.660
Androids going to a café?

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.870
They might not be here to drink tea.

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.330
More importantly, that rule.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:40.040
Seems pretty dangerous.

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:43.790
Under robot legislation, this is …

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:45.040
Under robot legislation, this is …
considered the grey zone.{Previous: This sits in the grey zone of robot law.}

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:46.170
It’s too vague.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:47.170
Listen to me!

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:48.040
It’s here.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:48.880
What?

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.170
Isn’t that the android from just now?

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:01.350
There’s no ring.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:01.350
You can’t be serious.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:03.980
Is this … in the grey zone?

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:06.350
Have you ever seen a robot without a ring?

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:09.900
This is a serious breach of robot legislation.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.690
You can’t even distinguish them from humans!

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:14.150
Who the hell is he?

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:17.610
Your order?

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:21.830
This. Eve Blend.

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:23.040
Evlend.

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:25.000
That.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:25.830
I’ll have one too.

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:26.870
Two Evlends!

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:30.790
Obey the rule, all right?

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:36.760
Ah. I just thought of something unpleasant.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:37.630
What?

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:39.140
This place’s rule.

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:41.220
“No discrimination between humans and robots”?

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:42.560
That’s why there’s no ring.

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:44.220
You don’t mean …!

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:47.140
Assuming that is true,

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:47.140
<i>Aki-chan,

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:48.400
<i>wake up!

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:49.400
he might not be the only problem.

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:50.400
What do you mean?

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:53.780
What if the other patrons are also obeying the rule …?

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:02.160
Those kids are looking at us.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:09.960
This is a dangerous café.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:10.670
Could be.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:11.630
This place …

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:13.670
What about it?

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:14.800
Tell your dad?

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:16.340
My old man has nothing to do with this!

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:19.340
What? You guys newbies?
I heard from Nagi-chan!

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.350
Nagi-chan is the girl at the counter.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:22.810
Anyway, can I sit here?

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:27.350
So, how did you find this place? Are you someone’s friend?

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:28.600
Uh no, we just stumbled across this place.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:31.150
Oh I see. Been a long time since we had newcomers!

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:33.520
Is that s—

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:33.520
It’s always the same people here.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:35.230
The one reading a book is Setoro-san, right?

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:36.610
On the second floor is Shimei-san and—

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:38.030
Ah! Cute little Chie-chan!

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:40.280
And over there are Kouji-kun and Rina-chan—aren’t they close?

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:41.490
And then there’s Nagi-chan, right?

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:42.990
And who else …

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.950
<i>The girl wearing a hat?</i>

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:44.950
Ah, I’m Akiko. Nice to meet you!

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:48.660
I’m Rikuo.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:49.290
Masaki.

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:51.920
Rikuo-kun and Masaki-kun.
I’ve remembered it!

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:53.840
Ah! Say, um,

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:55.590
have you seen a female android around?

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:56.880
<i>One demerit point.</i>

00:10:58.510 --> 00:10:59.550
You broke the rule.

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:02.340
Oh. Sorry.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.340
You don’t have to say it like that;

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.050
he’s not used to it yet.

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:05.970
But—

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:09.270
Infringing the rule troubles everyone!

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:11.400
Nagi-chan.

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:14.520
She’s really a good person.

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:16.530
She cares about this place a lot.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:18.490
Isn’t it just her job?

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:24.700
Oh no, she’s sulking again.

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:27.950
Nagi-chan!

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.910
I guess it doesn’t feel like a bad place.

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:33.580
Rather, I’d like to ask:

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:35.500
Why did she come to a place like this?

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:36.090
“Why”?

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:37.340
I know.

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:40.950
She wants to befriend robots.

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:42.150
She’s a dori-kei.

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:43.550
You disagree?

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:45.050
Um. I don’t think it’s like that.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:47.060
Well. You’d get it just by listening to her.

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:49.560
We’ll be told off again for breaking the rule.

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:52.190
There’s more than one way of asking.

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:53.310
Oh. Here she comes.

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:55.940
I brought them back!

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:57.520
She says she’s sorry.

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:04.700
Uh, I was in the wrong too!

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:06.700
What’cha shy about?{tried to shorten it, made it more casual to fit line length}

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:08.830
<i>That hat suits you.</i>

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:11.210
<i>Doesn’t it? I think so too!</i>

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:13.250
So … ahem.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:14.120
What is it?

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:16.880
Well, it’s something about the world outside this café.

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:18.630
There are no androids at my place.

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:20.130
Isn’t that uncommon in today’s world?

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:25.470
You have them too, don’t you? At your place?

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:31.480
Yep. We do. That’s why I’m here.

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:35.100
What do you mean?

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:37.770
Here, you can talk to anyone as you please, right?

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:40.230
So you get to know a lot of things.

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:40.980
Like what?

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:44.490
Such as … their feelings, maybe?

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:46.950
This has to do with my situation at home.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:51.200
I think of both humans and androids as my family.

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:54.000
But no matter how similar we appear to each other,

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.080
we’re totally different on the inside.

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:59.250
We may look alike, but we’re totally different.

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:01.340
So I often wonder,

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:04.720
“What do you think of me?”

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:08.970
That’s why I’m here.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:12.720
I want to be able to speak with them
and understand more about them.

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:14.890
Because we are family after all.

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:23.400
Bah. It’s pouring outside.

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:27.530
Isn’t your android coming to get you?

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:31.290
I want to know what they’re thinking about.

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:32.120
What do you think?

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:33.750
Regarding Akiko-san.

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:35.210
Ah, yesterday’s discussion.

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:38.250
Does someone like her count as a dori-kei?

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:41.130
I don’t have any androids, so I wouldn’t know.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:43.340
<i>Here’s your umbrella, Mas—

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:44.970
She’s way better than someone who uses them like that anyway.

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:52.100
Could she be … someone who treasures things?

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:53.470
Well, she’s a nice person, right?

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:54.640
That’s true, isn’t it?

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:56.440
What are you relieved about?

00:13:56.440 --> 00:13:58.270
No, it’s not like that.

00:13:58.270 --> 00:13:59.980
Well. She certainly was cute.

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:00.980
Should we go again today?

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:02.070
I said, it’s not like th—

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:11.330
Aki—

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:24.250
<i>I’m very sorry for making you wait, Mas—</i>

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:27.380
<i>My bag. Waterproof it. Go on by yourself.</i>

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:28.550
<i>Acknowledged.</i>

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:48.360
Here is your coffee.

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:56.750
As I thought. It tastes like that coffee.

00:14:57.870 --> 00:14:59.370
I used a different brand.

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:01.120
Huh? I gave no such command!

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:02.630
I thought Master would be pleased.

00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:07.510
Why did you decide that on your own?

00:15:08.590 --> 00:15:10.260
Are you trying to imitate humans too?

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:12.010
I am an android.

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:14.640
Not a human, Master.

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:22.730
<i>“What do you think of me?”</i>

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:25.770
<i>But no matter how similar we appear to each other,</i>

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:26.400
<b>Resembles</b>

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:27.440
<i>we are totally different on the inside.</i>

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:27.400
The other way round?

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:30.320
<i>We may look alike, but we’re totally different.</i>

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:30.820
<b></b>Akiko’s Point of View

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:30.820
<b>Resembles</b>

00:15:30.820 --> 00:15:32.530
<i>I want to understand more about them.</i>

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.830
<i>Because we are family after all.</i>

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:44.500
Hey. You’re acting like a dori-kei.

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:00.640
I have returned.

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:14.280
<i>Isn’t that it?</i>

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:15.160
<i>Ah. I remember it.</i>

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:16.490
<i>It’s here at last!</i>

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:18.580
<i>I have brought your change of clothes, Master.</i>

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:19.240
<i>Thank you.</i>

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:21.580
<i>Huh? You thanked it?</i>

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:22.830
<i>I don’t know. Am I not supposed to?</i>

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:23.750
<i>No way.</i>

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:24.960
<i>Eh, she’s a dori-kei?</i>

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:25.580
<i>What was that?!</i>

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:28.040
I’m telling you. It said it didn’t go to that café.

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:29.050
<i>Did you do anything aside from your orders?</i>

00:16:29.050 --> 00:16:30.300
<i>I did not.</i>

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:31.800
What about that?

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.010
The thing on your mobile phone.

00:16:33.010 --> 00:16:33.840
The movement records?

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:34.510
Yeah, that.

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:35.930
Could it be a problem on this end?

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:38.890
That’s why I say you trust robots too much.

00:16:38.890 --> 00:16:40.310
Then are you saying Sammy’s lying?

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:40.720
Sa—?

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:44.480
… Nothing.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:46.690
That’s our android.

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:46.690
So it’s called Sammy, huh?

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:48.650
Well. It’s not that I’m really trusting it.

00:16:49.150 --> 00:16:50.940
Why don’t you recite the three laws of robotics?

00:16:50.940 --> 00:16:52.110
We’re not in law school.

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:54.150
Stop arguing and just remember it already, Master.

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:55.860
Um. Firstly,

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:57.990
Do not inflict harm upon humans.

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:58.740
<b>First Law:</b>    A robot may not injure a human being or, through inaction,
allow a human being to come to harm.

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:58.740
Three Laws of Robotics

00:16:57.990 --> 00:16:58.740
That’s right.

00:16:58.740 --> 00:16:59.830
Secondly,

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:03.830
<b>Second Law:</b>    A robot must obey orders given to it by human beings,
except where such orders would conflict with the First Law.

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:03.830
Three Laws of Robotics

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:02.870
Obey commands from humans, unless this contravenes the first rule.

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:03.830
Oh. You do remember after all.

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:05.000
Thirdly—

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:07.210
Was there a third?

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:09.790
Protect yourself.

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:14.170
<b>Third Law:</b>    A robot must protect its own existence, as long as such protection does not conflict with the First or Second Law.

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:14.170
Three Laws of Robotics

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:11.630
These are the codes of conduct for a robot.

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:13.010
In other words, that’s all there is to it.

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:14.170
What do you mean?

00:17:14.170 --> 00:17:15.180
That is to say,

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:17.550
there’s no law against lying.

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:18.680
<b>First Law</b>:    Do not inflict harm upon humans

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:18.680
<b>Second Law</b>:    Obey human commands

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:18.680
<b>Third Law</b>:    Protect self

00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:19.350
No way!

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:21.060
It’s surprising how you don’t know this.

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:24.140
So, no matter what Sammy-chan says,

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:26.140
you can’t trust it fully.

00:17:26.690 --> 00:17:28.190
It goes shopping this afternoon, right?

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:29.110
What?

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:30.480
I’m talking about Sammy-chan!

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:32.230
Just shopping, it said.

00:17:32.230 --> 00:17:36.610
There’s no choice but to confirm for ourselves if that is the truth.

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:40.990
We’re going in after all?

00:17:40.990 --> 00:17:42.740
What is it now? Don’t you want to confirm it?

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:43.750
That’s not it.

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:44.750
Then that’s fine.

00:17:45.620 --> 00:17:46.540
Hm! All right.

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:47.330
What’s with that?

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:48.080
I said I’m going!

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:48.580
Then let’s go.

00:17:48.580 --> 00:17:49.330
Ah. Let’s go.

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:52.750
Ah. Welcome.

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:55.260
Hm?

00:17:55.630 --> 00:17:56.510
What’s up?

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:02.970
It’s too early to be relieved.

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:05.390
It might come in later.

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:10.900
We’re going to wait here?

00:18:10.900 --> 00:18:13.020
For now, shall we stick around until dinner?

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:14.860
But I don’t really want to confront it here.

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:22.870
Isn’t it all right here?

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:25.620
But I’m short-sighted.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:26.870
You can’t see it, even from here?

00:18:26.870 --> 00:18:29.000
After all, I can’t see anything if I take my glasses—

00:18:39.470 --> 00:18:41.890
Chie-chan! Come down from there!

00:18:41.890 --> 00:18:43.310
I’m a cat!

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:47.140
Oh. I’m so sorry, lads.

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:50.150
It’s best for kids to play around freely.

00:18:50.150 --> 00:18:51.350
Isn’t it?

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:52.610
Ah, freely.

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:54.360
Come along. Give it up now.

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.190
Those round things are weird.

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:58.860
Chie. There’s something you want to eat, isn’t there?

00:18:58.860 --> 00:19:00.030
I am a cat!

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:01.410
And ice-cream!

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:04.030
If you don’t sit down, you won’t get to eat it.

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:05.780
Heh. Sorry about that.

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:08.910
Once something catches her attention, she forgets all else.

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:14.540
It’s a piano.

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:15.250
Are you going to play it?

00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:16.760
I told you. I’ve quit playing.

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:18.010
I’ll play it.

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:23.510
That old man and the kid are both human, right?

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:25.390
No matter how you look at them, they’re human. But you know—

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:27.100
What?

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:29.850
Akiko appears to be human here as well.

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:31.440
Huh?

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:32.440
How lonely.

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:33.690
I will be lonely too.

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:35.150
Can’t we meet every day?

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:36.780
No. I have my job.

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:38.650
You are meeting other women outside, aren’t you?

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:40.030
No. That’s not it.

00:19:40.530 --> 00:19:42.780
Please don’t leave me by myself.

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:44.280
Yes. I know.

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:49.870
Shall we go somewhere else?

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:52.120
What were you doing?

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:53.120
Socializing.

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:54.040
Order something already.

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:55.040
Two blends.

00:19:55.040 --> 00:19:56.670
Evlends for two!

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:03.660
<i>It’s ice cream!</i>

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:05.550
<i>Here. Sorry, Chie-chan.</i>

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:07.930
<i>A cat’s tongue can take the cold.</i>

00:20:07.930 --> 00:20:09.890
You’re scared, aren’t you?

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:12.600
Scared of what?

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:13.940
Scared that Sammy-chan will come.

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:16.060
What are you on about?

00:20:16.060 --> 00:20:19.480
That your robot is someplace unknown,
doing whatever it wants.

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:21.950
“Violating human authority!”

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:23.280
Is that it?

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:23.280
Your dad said that?

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:25.320
Well. I don’t know.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:26.410
Don’t even have one.

00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:28.450
Why don’t you?

00:20:36.290 --> 00:20:37.790
<i>Ah. You’re here.</i>

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:37.790
<i>I’m here!</i>

00:20:44.630 --> 00:20:46.180
I don’t get why Sammy would lie.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:49.220
I find it harder to make sense of ring-less robots.

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:50.180
I’ll be right back.

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:00.650
Rikuo-kun!

00:21:02.240 --> 00:21:03.990
What? What’s up?

00:21:03.990 --> 00:21:05.240
Eh? What?

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:05.910
You’re completely different.

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:07.820
Heh? Really? I’m so happy!

00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:11.040
Uh, by the wa—

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:13.080
That’s not it. Question.

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:14.710
Where’s the ring on top of your head?

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:18.080
It’s off. If I don’t look human, it’ll be a rule violation.

00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:18.710
But the robot laws—

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:20.340
All right. This conversation ends here.

00:21:26.220 --> 00:21:27.430
Ah, Chie-chan!

00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:28.600
I’m a cat.

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:32.060
Chie-chan. What’s up?

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:32.060
He’s wearing something weird.

00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:33.020
Something weird?

00:21:57.420 --> 00:21:59.540
What do you want of me, Blade Runner?

00:22:00.380 --> 00:22:02.630
Shouldn’t you do a better job of observing me?

00:22:02.630 --> 00:22:04.710
Having a man’s intense gaze on me …

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:06.630
is far from comfortable.

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:07.720
I’m sorry.

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:13.520
Violate the rules, and you won’t have fun here.

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:16.890
No; rather, it would be meaningless to be here.

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:18.900
That’s what I’ve been told.

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:21.480
I don’t have it!

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:24.440
<i>Ah. Is today all right?</i>

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:27.070
Well. Please give my regards to your friend.

00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:34.080
<i>You’ll get them back eventually, probably.</i>

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:34.660
<i>Probably?</i>

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:36.410
<i>She hid my hat before as well.</i>

00:22:36.410 --> 00:22:37.910
My glasses got taken.

00:22:37.910 --> 00:22:39.120
<i>So where was your hat?</i>

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:39.750
<i>My hat?</i>

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:40.540
<i>She hid it right?</i>

00:22:40.540 --> 00:22:45.420
<i>Ah, that’s right. And then I asked where it was, and Chie-chan had it all along!</i>

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:45.420
<i>Please come again!</i>

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:49.090
She had it?

00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:50.550
She hid it inside her panties!

00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:53.890
She must have taken a liking to it.

00:22:53.890 --> 00:22:55.600
Sorry. Something came up.

00:22:56.270 --> 00:22:57.390
What a nice atmosphere, huh.

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:00.520
What’s up with Masaki-kun?

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:02.980
Dunno.

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:09.820
It won’t open now.

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:10.700
What?

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:13.160
When someone leaves,
it stays locked for some time.

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:14.080
Why?

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:16.080
Privacy is precious.

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:27.170
<i>Um. Is this size all right?</i>

00:23:27.170 --> 00:23:27.800
<i>Yup.</i>

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:29.340
<i>We have larger sizes.</i>

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:32.300
<i>If it’s too big, the other household members will find out.</i>

00:23:32.300 --> 00:23:34.800
<i>All right then. Hold on a second.</i>

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:36.470
<i>I’ll get you a good blend.</i>

00:23:44.810 --> 00:23:45.980
What should we do, Rina-chan?

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:48.820
I’m really sorry.
Please leave first.

00:23:51.400 --> 00:23:52.780
Here. Today’s coffee.

00:23:52.780 --> 00:23:55.200
Sorry for always taking things from your café.

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:56.160
Did they like it?

00:23:57.040 --> 00:23:59.290
I wonder if they did.

00:23:59.660 --> 00:24:01.370
You could always ask them yourself.

00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:02.120
I can’t do that!

00:24:02.120 --> 00:24:02.870
Why?

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:06.790
He hates it when I try to start things.

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:09.170
“Don’t do things on your own,” he’d say.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:10.880
It’s all right.

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:13.840
He will definitely understand you.

00:24:11.720 --> 00:24:13.840
<i>Well, isn’t this café’s rule a local one?</i>

00:24:14.050 --> 00:24:15.180
You can’t compare it with the robot laws.

00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:17.310
You’re violating the rule again. You’ll get caught.

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:17.810
You won’t say no matter what?

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:21.020
I’ll keep it a secret from Nagi-chan, so this conversation ends here.

00:24:21.730 --> 00:24:22.520
Secret?

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:26.020
So you really can keep secrets.

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:28.980
Quite a strange conversation you’re having.

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:32.240
Everyone has a thing or two that they’re hiding.

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:33.950
Well, but she’s an—

00:24:33.950 --> 00:24:36.450
<b>In this café,</b>

00:24:33.950 --> 00:24:36.450
there is to be no discrimination

00:24:33.950 --> 00:24:36.450
<b>between humans and robots.</b>

00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:36.450
Ah, umm …

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:40.240
I’m also keeping something from this girl.

00:24:40.240 --> 00:24:42.500
I’m not saying that I won’t lie to her either.

00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:46.580
But it’s for this child’s sake.

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:49.460
Akiko-san is keeping that secret of yours …

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:51.250
for your sake too, isn’t it?

00:24:52.970 --> 00:24:54.050
<i>“Do not inflict harm upon humans.”</i>

00:24:54.050 --> 00:24:55.220
<i>“These are the codes of conduct for a robot.”</i>

00:24:55.220 --> 00:24:56.180
<i>“Did you do anything besides your orders—”</i>

00:24:56.180 --> 00:24:57.180
<i>“I did not.”</i>

00:24:57.760 --> 00:24:59.260
Because I will get hurt.

00:25:00.260 --> 00:25:01.220
… For my sake?

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:03.140
To begin with,

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:06.100
she didn’t tell you where your glasses were because …

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:09.610
haha, she just wanted to play with you, isn’t it?

00:25:09.610 --> 00:25:10.860
They’re not in her panties?

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:13.490
She seems to have handed it to someone.

00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:20.120
Oh. It’s already this late.

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:20.910
Are you heading back?

00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:22.660
I have to prepare dinner.

00:25:22.660 --> 00:25:24.250
Thanks for this.

00:25:24.250 --> 00:25:25.000
Hey.

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:26.750
Ah. You finally realized.

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:28.250
Why did you keep quiet about it?

00:25:28.250 --> 00:25:29.670
You didn’t ask.

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:32.300
So the customers and staff here are chummy huh?

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:13.250
What?

00:26:13.250 --> 00:26:14.510
Damn it!

00:26:13.630 --> 00:26:14.510
Rikuo-kun.

00:26:14.510 --> 00:26:15.970
What’s this? Dammit!

00:26:15.970 --> 00:26:17.180
Rikuo-kun.

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:18.630
… Doing things on her own!

00:26:19.430 --> 00:26:20.180
She’s my—!

00:26:20.180 --> 00:26:21.260
Sit down!

00:26:24.020 --> 00:26:24.970
… Yes.

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:38.280
This cup’s on the house.

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:45.700
That girl …

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:48.710
when did she first arrive?

00:26:48.710 --> 00:26:50.880
She was a bit confused at first,

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:53.090
but she gradually warmed to us.

00:26:53.090 --> 00:26:54.090
Warmed to you?

00:26:54.090 --> 00:26:56.880
Yup. She was able to talk to us about many things.

00:26:57.340 --> 00:26:59.760
The mother who buys her clothes.

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:02.050
Her occasional nightmares.

00:27:02.050 --> 00:27:04.310
The boy who would not play the piano any more.

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:07.350
Just the other day, she came in.

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:09.560
Said she wanted to make good coffee.

00:27:11.310 --> 00:27:13.360
She seems troubled by many things.

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:14.570
Troubled?

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:16.230
You don’t understand, do you?

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:19.200
Whether the things one does are right,

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:20.530
or wrong.

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:22.780
Here.

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:30.120
As long as you are following the rule,

00:27:30.500 --> 00:27:34.130
Time of Eve is always open to welcome you.

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:54.900
So, in the end, I lost sight of him there.

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:56.070
You followed him outside?

00:27:56.070 --> 00:27:58.780
I wanted to check if he had anything above his head.

00:27:58.780 --> 00:28:01.740
That café is definitely up to something—

00:28:00.700 --> 00:28:01.740
Um.

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:05.450
I was thinking of going there again sometime.

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:07.030
Oh. I’ll come with you!

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:09.000
This time we’ll definitely unravel this mystery!

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:13.790
All right. See you then.
Say “hi” to Robot Sammy-chan for me.

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:22.800
Question.

00:28:23.180 --> 00:28:24.090
What is it?

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:26.010
Um, that café …

00:28:30.270 --> 00:28:31.890
Master, I am sorr—

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:32.980
Coffee.

00:28:32.980 --> 00:28:34.190
Acknowledged.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:38.980
Recently, the coffee’s …

00:28:39.820 --> 00:28:41.780
been quite delicious, I think.

00:28:44.030 --> 00:28:45.410
Thank you very much.

00:28:49.580 --> 00:28:52.290
<i>I saw a lot of interesting things.</i>

00:28:52.290 --> 00:28:54.000
<i>But I haven’t pinned down anything specific.</i>

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:55.830
Anything to do with the Ethics Committee?

00:28:55.830 --> 00:28:58.630
If there were, they wouldn’t have used such a careless shadow.

00:28:58.630 --> 00:29:01.420
I’ll let it swim free for a while, see where that takes us.

00:29:01.420 --> 00:29:02.510
Indeed.

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:05.720
Chairwoman Ashimori.

00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:09.220
As it is, it seems individuality is beginning to manifest.

00:29:09.220 --> 00:29:10.890
This means the suppression circuit is—

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:13.310
“Assumptions distract from the truth.”

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:16.020
That’s what Shiotsuki would say.

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:18.690
I will continue my duties.

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:13.540
<b>Love isn’t present there.</b>

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:14.540
<b>Ethics Committee</b>

00:30:15.540 --> 00:30:18.540
Hasn’t this commercial been appearing really often lately?

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:22.340
Status: Rikuo.

00:30:22.340 --> 00:30:25.550
12:03 p.m.: Left the house.

00:30:25.550 --> 00:30:27.430
Having lunch outside.

00:30:29.390 --> 00:30:31.550
I’m just saying,

00:30:31.550 --> 00:30:33.810
but you come here really often {when you’re free}in the afternoons, don’t you?{Southrop-edit}

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:37.060
Hmm … Skipping classes?

00:30:37.310 --> 00:30:38.890
Compensatory holiday.

00:30:38.890 --> 00:30:40.560
That’s not something a café assistant should say.

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:45.070
Sorry. Rikuo-kun aside, I thought
Masaki-kun was a bit of a delinquent.

00:30:45.070 --> 00:30:47.450
He’s on a scholarship. Quite unexpected.

00:30:47.450 --> 00:30:48.950
Don’t say anything unnecessary!

00:30:48.950 --> 00:30:50.070
It’s not something to hide anyway.

00:30:50.450 --> 00:30:54.080
Hey. If you’re looking for someone to talk to, there are other people.

00:30:54.580 --> 00:30:56.080
<i>Do you always carry that with you?</i>

00:30:56.080 --> 00:30:58.080
<i>Yeah. I take various pictures with it.</i>

00:30:58.080 --> 00:30:59.460
Well. We’ll get around to it eventually.

00:30:59.460 --> 00:31:00.290
Thanks for the meal.

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:03.590
You’re welcome. I’ll bring your after-meal Evlend over.

00:31:05.460 --> 00:31:06.960
Evlend. Evlend.

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:08.050
What?

00:31:08.050 --> 00:31:09.880
I’ve stopped feeling uncomfortable about it!

00:31:09.880 --> 00:31:12.680
<i>Ah! Not my reading glasses!</i>

00:31:12.680 --> 00:31:14.600
<i>Chie! Give it back!</i>

00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:15.600
<i>I’m a cat!</i>

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:18.350
There’s no point just sitting around.

00:31:18.350 --> 00:31:21.100
Well. I just want to know what this café is all about.

00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:23.730
I’ve no intention of getting close to any of the robots.

00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:25.940
We don’t know about anyone other than Akiko-san.

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:27.280
What about them, to my right?

00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:31.110
A dori-kei getting intimate with his own android.

00:31:32.370 --> 00:31:35.120
No further explanation needed.

00:31:38.450 --> 00:31:39.750
Where are you staring?

00:31:39.750 --> 00:31:41.170
Come on.

00:31:40.370 --> 00:31:41.170
I wasn’t staring!

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:42.210
No, you were—

00:31:41.620 --> 00:31:42.210
I’m saying I wasn’t!

00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:43.330
You stared.

00:31:42.790 --> 00:31:43.330
I wasn’t staring!

00:31:43.330 --> 00:31:44.290
You were staring.

00:31:43.790 --> 00:31:44.290
I wasn’t staring!

00:31:44.290 --> 00:31:45.460
You were staring!

00:31:44.290 --> 00:31:45.460
<i>You two sure get along well.</i>

00:31:45.460 --> 00:31:46.630
I’ve never been told that before!

00:31:45.460 --> 00:31:46.630
No way!

00:31:46.630 --> 00:31:49.130
Just two boys getting all excited is pretty lonely you know.

00:31:49.130 --> 00:31:50.300
The next time I catch you staring—

00:31:50.300 --> 00:31:51.130
I wasn’t staring!

00:31:51.130 --> 00:31:52.090
At what?

00:31:53.140 --> 00:31:54.640
So you’re jealous of Kouji-kun.

00:31:54.640 --> 00:31:55.510
I’m not!

00:31:55.510 --> 00:31:57.600
He’s just very interesting, right?

00:31:59.310 --> 00:32:00.100
Hey!

00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:01.100
Why don’t you join us here?

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:12.700
Wouldn’t it be better not to do anything unnecessary?

00:32:12.700 --> 00:32:13.950
Was it unnecessary, really?

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:16.030
I think it would have made more sense to let things develop on their own.{TLC - consistent?}{i think he says suji 筋 which means reasonable/logical amongst other meanings, so changing to reflect that --rop}

00:32:16.030 --> 00:32:18.540
Four people will stimulate more conversation, right?

00:32:37.720 --> 00:32:41.230
He’s Kouji. I’m Rina.

00:32:40.060 --> 00:32:41.230
Ah, nice to meet you.

00:32:44.850 --> 00:32:46.560
I’m Masaki. This guy here is Rikuo.

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:47.860
Next time you stare, I’ll smack you.

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:49.150
I wasn’t staring!

00:32:49.480 --> 00:32:50.780
Was I?

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:50.780
Subconsciously huh?

00:32:50.780 --> 00:32:52.030
You two …

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:52.030
You two sure get along well.

00:32:52.030 --> 00:32:53.400
No way!

00:32:52.030 --> 00:32:53.400
Never heard that before!

00:32:53.650 --> 00:32:54.910
Or have I?

00:33:12.050 --> 00:33:14.430
Ah. You sure come here often.

00:33:17.050 --> 00:33:17.930
Yes.

00:33:24.310 --> 00:33:25.940
Do you two come here together?

00:33:25.940 --> 00:33:27.270
No. Separately.

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:36.200
It sure is going splendidly, huh?

00:33:37.070 --> 00:33:39.370
Ah, is that the Cinema Tide?

00:33:41.740 --> 00:33:42.950
Can I have a look?

00:33:46.370 --> 00:33:47.420
<i>Does he like it?

00:33:46.670 --> 00:33:47.420
The switch is there.

00:33:47.420 --> 00:33:48.170
<i>What?

00:33:48.170 --> 00:33:50.210
Ah, it’s beautiful.

00:33:50.210 --> 00:33:51.170
Can you see a preview of it?

00:33:51.170 --> 00:33:53.380
Yes, if you press that triangle over there.

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:53.380
<i>He’s just making conversation, isn’t he?

00:33:54.300 --> 00:33:55.670
It’s got 500.

00:33:57.380 --> 00:33:58.760
Oh. That’s right.

00:33:59.260 --> 00:34:01.140
It has M-link, so maybe …

00:34:15.280 --> 00:34:17.150
Um, hold on …

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:26.410
Cine-Tide sure is awesome after all!

00:34:26.410 --> 00:34:28.920
Um. That’s about it, so …

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:29.960
How do I turn this off?

00:34:29.960 --> 00:34:30.880
What is this?

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:48.770
It’s not what you think.

00:34:51.730 --> 00:34:54.730
Your heart … it’s so distant.

00:34:55.780 --> 00:34:58.700
No matter how many times we meet,
or how intimate we get,

00:34:58.700 --> 00:34:59.820
I feel uneasy.

00:35:00.820 --> 00:35:03.280
I finally understand why.

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:10.960
Please don’t leave me alone.

00:35:13.090 --> 00:35:13.960
That’s quite the drama.

00:35:13.960 --> 00:35:15.710
My fault … I guess?

00:35:15.710 --> 00:35:16.960
It is your fault.

00:35:16.960 --> 00:35:18.420
Sorry about the wait!

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:20.090
Wh-what?

00:35:21.050 --> 00:35:22.470
What is it?

00:35:22.470 --> 00:35:24.350
I’ll go take care of things.

00:35:24.890 --> 00:35:26.100
What’s wrong?

00:35:26.100 --> 00:35:28.640
Well. What do you call this? A scene of carnage?

00:36:11.350 --> 00:36:12.730
<i>Protected witness has arrived.</i>

00:36:20.440 --> 00:36:23.110
I was deployed as my Master’s guard.

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:25.120
Then, Kouji is …?

00:36:26.830 --> 00:36:28.370
What have I been doing until now?!

00:36:28.370 --> 00:36:30.500
It seems the emergency repairs were incomplete.

00:36:31.250 --> 00:36:33.540
You came here for Kouji’s, uh—

00:36:33.540 --> 00:36:35.250
your Master’s protection?

00:36:35.250 --> 00:36:37.540
No. I took my own initiative.

00:36:37.540 --> 00:36:40.010
That leg; shouldn’t it be looked at by the maker?

00:36:43.380 --> 00:36:47.510
I cannot have anything to do with my legal maker.

00:36:47.510 --> 00:36:49.970
If my maker were to find out about me,

00:36:49.970 --> 00:36:52.520
my Master will be put in a disadvantageous position.

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:57.020
That aside, I would like to be here a little longer too.

00:36:57.020 --> 00:36:59.020
Not even going for repairs.

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:05.070
In this café, I’m the one who acts least like a human.

00:37:05.070 --> 00:37:06.370
Am I right?

00:37:08.080 --> 00:37:11.750
I wouldn’t be surprised if I were destroyed sooner rather than later.

00:37:12.660 --> 00:37:18.460
That is why I am autonomously resisting my useless learning functions.

00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:22.630
Kouji treated someone like me …

00:37:22.630 --> 00:37:25.180
like a human female.

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:30.060
I think that’s why I am happy here.

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:32.930
But, the woman in that photograph …

00:37:32.930 --> 00:37:34.480
You’re kind, aren’t you?

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:36.730
But it’ll be all right,

00:37:36.730 --> 00:37:39.570
because I think Kouji’s kindness will not change.

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:45.490
I guess …

00:37:45.990 --> 00:37:47.070
I’m going back.

00:37:52.580 --> 00:37:53.580
Excuse me!

00:37:55.410 --> 00:37:56.330
The coffee—

00:37:59.330 --> 00:38:01.710
When my coffee’s flavor changed,

00:38:01.710 --> 00:38:03.340
I was angry at first.

00:38:03.710 --> 00:38:06.380
Then, after I understood various things, I was a little glad,

00:38:06.380 --> 00:38:09.050
though she isn’t human …

00:38:10.350 --> 00:38:11.350
Tell him!

00:38:11.350 --> 00:38:13.930
Tell him what you want to say, even if he’s a human!

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:18.100
Why … do you think that?

00:38:24.650 --> 00:38:28.030
You knew, didn’t you, your feelings at that moment?

00:38:28.410 --> 00:38:29.620
Probably.

00:38:30.950 --> 00:38:34.040
How did I end up saying such things?

00:38:35.660 --> 00:38:37.210
So you’re serious about this one?

00:38:39.670 --> 00:38:41.460
Something’s been bothering me since just now.

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:45.420
There is not a single picture of you with Rina.

00:38:45.420 --> 00:38:47.260
But I guess you couldn’t have such a thing.

00:38:47.260 --> 00:38:49.720
It would be bad if people knew you had an imaginary lover.

00:38:50.300 --> 00:38:52.550
No; if this one is a robot too—

00:38:52.550 --> 00:38:53.430
She’s not a robot!

00:38:53.810 --> 00:38:55.430
All the more reason then.

00:38:55.430 --> 00:38:56.560
What are you doing here?

00:38:56.560 --> 00:38:58.020
She’s not my imaginary lover!

00:38:59.270 --> 00:39:01.020
Then are you saying this relationship is for real?

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:03.980
“There is love.”

00:39:03.980 --> 00:39:05.650
That’s not what you’re trying to say, is it?

00:39:12.450 --> 00:39:14.410
What you’re trying to say is,

00:39:14.790 --> 00:39:17.910
“For humans, a human partner is best.”

00:39:17.910 --> 00:39:19.410
So you do understand that.

00:39:21.880 --> 00:39:22.960
I understand.

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:25.130
I know that.

00:39:26.380 --> 00:39:31.090
As you said, Rina and I cannot transcend a human–android relationship.

00:39:31.090 --> 00:39:32.850
I think there are things we cannot understand about each other.

00:39:32.850 --> 00:39:34.350
There’s no way you could understand each other.

00:39:36.140 --> 00:39:37.060
You see.

00:39:38.390 --> 00:39:40.390
I want to help my Master.

00:39:47.480 --> 00:39:50.610
My Master is very much like Rina.

00:39:50.610 --> 00:39:53.120
I want to help my Master.

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:01.920
Yesterday too; I saw a lot of people.

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:04.670
I don’t care about that.

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:12.430
But, Master.

00:40:14.300 --> 00:40:17.680
Master, you should look for a new partner.

00:40:17.680 --> 00:40:19.020
A human—

00:40:21.940 --> 00:40:24.770
That’s enough. I’m tired.

00:40:24.770 --> 00:40:26.190
You’re enough, Kouji.

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:28.860
I’m going to become a robot.

00:40:29.780 --> 00:40:31.450
So stay with me always.

00:40:32.450 --> 00:40:34.820
My Master is morbidly dependent on me.

00:40:34.820 --> 00:40:37.410
But I don’t know what to do about that.

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:41.830
When I first met Rina, I thought she was just like my Master.

00:40:42.210 --> 00:40:43.830
The way she behaves and touches me,

00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:45.710
and the fleeting expressions she sometimes shows me.

00:40:45.710 --> 00:40:48.960
I want to know more about Rina, to better understand my Master.

00:40:49.710 --> 00:40:51.470
I want to help my Master.

00:40:52.340 --> 00:40:53.720
The First Law!

00:40:54.930 --> 00:40:58.100
About all this; can you keep it from Rina?

00:40:59.720 --> 00:41:00.970
The café’s rule?

00:41:03.100 --> 00:41:05.730
I don’t want to hurt anyone.

00:41:05.980 --> 00:41:07.400
Convenient for you huh?

00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:10.030
Aren’t you a failure?

00:41:12.490 --> 00:41:15.070
It seems no one can really attain happiness.

00:41:29.090 --> 00:41:29.960
Thank you.

00:41:31.880 --> 00:41:35.010
<i>We should</i>

00:41:33.090 --> 00:41:35.010
<i>get out of here.</i>

00:41:35.800 --> 00:41:37.590
In that case, please excuse us.

00:41:44.940 --> 00:41:46.690
I guess there are all sorts.

00:41:47.190 --> 00:41:49.480
Of humans?

00:41:47.940 --> 00:41:49.480
Androids too.

00:41:52.530 --> 00:41:54.070
Sorry about hitting you earlier.

00:41:54.530 --> 00:41:56.530
Your eyes were right.

00:41:56.530 --> 00:41:58.030
I should have taken a good look myself.

00:42:00.950 --> 00:42:01.830
Masaki.

00:42:03.250 --> 00:42:04.250
Have you awoken?

00:42:04.250 --> 00:42:05.910
I’m not a pubescent junior high student!

00:42:15.840 --> 00:42:17.880
But, Rina is—

00:42:16.590 --> 00:42:17.880
No! It’s the other way round, isn’t it?

00:42:26.310 --> 00:42:27.190
Hold on.

00:42:27.190 --> 00:42:28.940
What did Rina say, again?

00:42:28.940 --> 00:42:31.400
<i>You came here for Kouji’s, uh—</i>

00:42:31.400 --> 00:42:33.230
<i>your Master’s protection?</i>

00:42:33.230 --> 00:42:33.940
<i>No.</i>

00:42:34.940 --> 00:42:38.280
She never once said her Master was Kouji.

00:42:35.490 --> 00:42:39.070
<b>Master</b>

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:39.160
She didn’t say it.

00:42:38.910 --> 00:42:42.530
<b>Master</b>

00:42:39.160 --> 00:42:41.990
They have nothing to do with each other outside of this café?

00:42:39.450 --> 00:42:42.530
<b>Master</b>

00:42:41.990 --> 00:42:43.950
But they both said the other is human.

00:42:42.950 --> 00:42:43.950
<b>Human</b>

00:42:42.950 --> 00:42:43.950
<b>Human</b>

00:42:44.330 --> 00:42:45.080
Something’s off—

00:42:45.080 --> 00:42:46.200
Here you go! Thanks for waiting.

00:42:48.170 --> 00:42:50.540
They might not have realized about each other.

00:42:51.130 --> 00:42:53.420
Yup. That’s it for hints.

00:42:55.210 --> 00:42:57.220
<b>Human</b>

00:42:55.210 --> 00:42:57.220
<b>Human</b>

00:42:57.220 --> 00:42:58.340
It’s the same!

00:42:58.340 --> 00:42:59.720
Will you please explain this properly?

00:42:59.720 --> 00:43:01.850
Partners not knowing what the other is;

00:43:01.850 --> 00:43:03.510
it’s the same with androids.

00:43:04.470 --> 00:43:06.890
They are interested in each other.

00:43:06.890 --> 00:43:08.230
For different reasons, that is.

00:43:08.230 --> 00:43:09.980
They also have things they hide from each other.

00:43:09.980 --> 00:43:11.190
So what?

00:43:11.610 --> 00:43:13.110
It’s like normal love.

00:43:13.730 --> 00:43:15.320
That should be a good thing, right?

00:43:15.320 --> 00:43:18.070
But don’t interfere too much.

00:43:19.240 --> 00:43:21.410
<i>Love between androids …</i>

00:43:21.410 --> 00:43:22.780
<i>Pseudo-love huh?</i>

00:43:22.120 --> 00:43:24.240
So that woman in the photo the other day.

00:43:24.240 --> 00:43:25.240
Who was that?

00:43:25.870 --> 00:43:29.160
A love triangle between androids.

00:43:27.500 --> 00:43:29.160
<i>It’s a bit hard to explain.</i>

00:43:29.160 --> 00:43:32.630
<i>A difficult-to-explain woman, huh?</i>

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:32.630
Androids tangled in blind love.

00:43:32.630 --> 00:43:34.250
Don’t think deviant thoughts!

00:43:34.250 --> 00:43:36.250
You’ll tell me about it properly someday, right?

00:43:35.820 --> 00:43:37.040
I will?

00:43:46.680 --> 00:43:48.890
Welcome home, Master.

00:43:49.770 --> 00:43:54.270
Nao-chan and Mom have gone out for dinner.
Sammy-chan, please take care of the rest!!

00:43:55.940 --> 00:43:57.780
What did you put on your head?

00:43:59.650 --> 00:44:01.030
Do you not like it?

00:45:37.960 --> 00:45:44.470
<b>Vagrant Robot Reporting Hotline</b>

00:45:37.960 --> 00:45:44.470
<b>Vagrant androids are dangerously likely to have corrupted thought logic. Report all discoveries immediately.</b>

00:45:40.340 --> 00:45:42.630
This is a request from the Ethics Committee.

00:45:43.550 --> 00:45:46.340
Currently, the Ethics Committee …

00:45:46.340 --> 00:45:49.430
is undertaking independent recovery of vagrant robots …

00:45:49.430 --> 00:45:51.470
that have been removed from the supervision of their Masters …

00:45:59.610 --> 00:46:03.110
What is this drawing of?

00:46:03.110 --> 00:46:05.320
You can’t recognise yourself?!

00:46:05.320 --> 00:46:07.360
This is me?

00:46:07.360 --> 00:46:09.490
That’s right! It is a drawing of ✲✲✲✲!

00:46:25.880 --> 00:46:27.130
Isn’t it quiet?

00:46:27.130 --> 00:46:28.760
You mean there are no customers.

00:46:28.760 --> 00:46:30.140
Oh, but …

00:46:29.260 --> 00:46:30.140
Huh?

00:46:30.140 --> 00:46:31.850
Have they gone back?

00:46:31.850 --> 00:46:33.640
This really is a strange café, isn’t it?

00:46:33.640 --> 00:46:34.850
In various ways.

00:46:34.850 --> 00:46:36.690
Ex-excuse me!

00:46:36.690 --> 00:46:38.900
Nagi-san, why do you work here?

00:46:38.900 --> 00:46:40.270
You can just call me Nagi.

00:46:40.270 --> 00:46:41.520
Because I like it here.

00:46:41.520 --> 00:46:42.900
I also like the people who come here.

00:46:43.900 --> 00:46:48.150
So on days like today, when I can’t see my favorite customers’ faces, I get lonely.

00:46:49.610 --> 00:46:51.030
Um, what about us …?

00:46:51.030 --> 00:46:52.490
Huh? What?

00:46:55.500 --> 00:46:57.710
This. Can I get a refill?

00:46:59.670 --> 00:47:02.210
I’ll just bring out the beans. Please wait a while.

00:47:02.540 --> 00:47:04.000
Enjoy your stay.

00:47:08.680 --> 00:47:10.640
It really is quiet, isn’t it?

00:47:32.870 --> 00:47:34.780
It’s an luh model.

00:47:35.240 --> 00:47:37.580
A houseroid model released when we were in primary school!

00:47:37.580 --> 00:47:38.500
It’s still around, huh?

00:47:38.500 --> 00:47:39.750
I’ve never seen one before.

00:47:39.750 --> 00:47:41.370
They weren’t very popular back then.

00:47:41.370 --> 00:47:43.250
There were many complaints that they were hard to use.

00:47:54.800 --> 00:47:56.930
It’s shaking pretty badly though.

00:48:11.070 --> 00:48:14.070
Do you have some business here?
We’re just customers.

00:48:14.070 --> 00:48:15.870
Do you understand what a “customer” is?

00:48:15.870 --> 00:48:17.790
I am also a “customer.”

00:48:22.170 --> 00:48:24.290
<i>I am Masaki. This guy is Rikuo.</i>

00:48:24.290 --> 00:48:25.380
<i>So. Who are you?</i>

00:48:25.380 --> 00:48:26.840
<i>Wh-what are you doing?</i>

00:48:27.300 --> 00:48:29.920
Normally, humans would introduce themselves.

00:48:30.880 --> 00:48:34.390
Introduction is a process wherein I communicate my name, is it not?

00:48:34.390 --> 00:48:36.350
This guy is trying to imitate humans.

00:48:36.350 --> 00:48:38.350
Heh. It’s abiding by the rule.

00:48:38.810 --> 00:48:41.350
<i>That’s right! It is a drawing of ✲✲✲✲!</i>

00:48:42.480 --> 00:48:43.590
I am called … ✲✲✲✲.

00:48:43.590 --> 00:48:45.560
I am called … ✲✲✲✲.

00:48:47.070 --> 00:48:48.980
Why did you cover your ears?

00:48:49.480 --> 00:48:53.820
If I do not communicate my name,
the introduction cannot be completed.

00:48:53.820 --> 00:48:58.200
In other words, I will be unable to verify my status as a human.

00:48:58.200 --> 00:49:01.040
My name is ✲✲✲✲.

00:49:01.040 --> 00:49:03.420
It-it’s all right! You don’t have to overdo it.

00:49:03.420 --> 00:49:04.580
Overdo what?

00:49:04.580 --> 00:49:05.830
The introduction!

00:49:05.830 --> 00:49:08.500
Why is that? Just a moment ago,

00:49:08.500 --> 00:49:11.170
<i>“Normally, humans would introduce themselves.”</i>

00:49:11.170 --> 00:49:13.010
… is what you said.

00:49:13.010 --> 00:49:15.340
Masaki! This one cannot deal with fuzzy interactions!

00:49:15.340 --> 00:49:16.510
What should we do?!

00:49:16.510 --> 00:49:18.010
Anyway, if we don’t let it introduce itself—

00:49:18.010 --> 00:49:18.680
What about that sound?!

00:49:18.680 --> 00:49:20.060
For the time being, we’ll have to bear with it.

00:49:20.470 --> 00:49:22.520
We’re sorry! Go ahead and say it!

00:49:22.520 --> 00:49:25.310
Like I said, I am ✲✲✲✲.

00:49:28.270 --> 00:49:30.440
It-it’s a nice name huh.

00:49:30.440 --> 00:49:32.360
I think so too.

00:49:32.360 --> 00:49:33.900
It’s very important to me.

00:49:33.900 --> 00:49:35.030
That’s wonderful.

00:49:40.490 --> 00:49:42.910
Where do I find a restroom?

00:49:57.430 --> 00:49:59.180
That broken machine,

00:49:59.180 --> 00:50:01.180
it was left alone, to fall into such disrepair.

00:50:01.180 --> 00:50:02.430
It should be returned to the maker for repairs—

00:50:02.430 --> 00:50:04.180
You’re talking like that again.

00:50:04.180 --> 00:50:05.690
But I think today—

00:50:05.690 --> 00:50:06.940
Rikuo-kun.

00:50:07.940 --> 00:50:09.060
He is a customer.

00:50:09.060 --> 00:50:10.570
You already understand that, don’t you?

00:50:10.570 --> 00:50:12.820
Everyone who comes here is the same.

00:50:15.200 --> 00:50:16.200
Excuse me.

00:50:16.200 --> 00:50:18.320
Welcome. What would you like to order?

00:50:21.700 --> 00:50:22.950
Something feels wrong.

00:50:22.950 --> 00:50:24.330
It might not be able to decide.

00:50:24.330 --> 00:50:25.620
This is something new for it after all.

00:50:29.500 --> 00:50:30.670
Ouch!

00:50:30.670 --> 00:50:32.420
Order! One more cup of this!

00:50:34.420 --> 00:50:37.050
I would like to order what Rikuo-sama is having.

00:50:37.050 --> 00:50:38.930
Yep. Two Evlends!

00:50:42.220 --> 00:50:43.560
Going to drink the coffee?

00:50:43.560 --> 00:50:45.020
Three cups might be pushing it.

00:50:45.020 --> 00:50:46.180
No. I meant the robot.

00:50:47.190 --> 00:50:48.940
That mouth is just a speaker.

00:50:48.940 --> 00:50:50.940
What if it tries to drink, imitating humans?

00:50:50.940 --> 00:50:52.110
If it does that …

00:50:54.730 --> 00:50:58.150
Hey. Hold on! The Third Law prohibits robot suicide!

00:50:55.440 --> 00:51:00.450
Three Laws of Robotics

00:50:55.440 --> 00:51:00.450
<b>Third Law</b>

00:50:55.440 --> 00:51:00.450
A robot must protect its own existence, as long as such protection
does not conflict with the First or Second Law.

00:50:58.150 --> 00:51:00.450
Does this guy not understand that drinking could be dangerous?

00:51:00.450 --> 00:51:04.620
That is true. It wouldn’t try to drink … right?

00:51:12.630 --> 00:51:14.380
Is something the matter?

00:51:14.960 --> 00:51:16.210
Coffee is … dangerous!

00:51:16.210 --> 00:51:17.970
Coffee is … dangerous!

00:51:20.720 --> 00:51:21.840
Wh-what are you doing?

00:51:22.600 --> 00:51:23.810
<i>What?</i>

00:51:23.810 --> 00:51:25.890
<i>Thanks for waiting on the Evlend!</i>

00:51:25.890 --> 00:51:26.720
<i>Why?!</i>

00:51:26.720 --> 00:51:28.980
<i>It’s for the customer to drink, isn’t it?</i>

00:51:28.980 --> 00:51:30.600
<i>Don’t say such weird things.</i>

00:51:35.860 --> 00:51:37.440
Hey, hold it! wait!

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:37.440
Cut the switch!

00:51:37.440 --> 00:51:39.650
I haven’t done this before!

00:51:38.360 --> 00:51:39.650
Or-order it! Just order it!

00:51:39.650 --> 00:51:40.360
Oh. I see!

00:51:40.360 --> 00:51:41.950
Drop the cup! It’s an order!

00:51:41.950 --> 00:51:42.870
Why?

00:51:43.530 --> 00:51:44.370
“Why”?

00:51:44.370 --> 00:51:45.540
She said,

00:51:45.540 --> 00:51:48.080
<i>“It’s for the customer to drink, isn’t it?</i>

00:51:48.080 --> 00:51:49.370
<i>“Don’t say such weird things.”</i>

00:51:49.710 --> 00:51:51.710
… a while ago.

00:51:51.710 --> 00:51:53.880
I am a customer.

00:51:55.380 --> 00:51:56.210
Ah! Question!

00:51:56.500 --> 00:51:57.550
What is it?

00:51:57.550 --> 00:51:58.550
Um.

00:51:59.470 --> 00:52:00.550
H-h-how old are you?

00:52:00.550 --> 00:52:04.050
Age-wise? This year I will be eight years old.

00:52:09.430 --> 00:52:11.690
He probably means that he was manufactured eight years ago.

00:52:11.690 --> 00:52:15.060
You thought I was a bit big to be an eight-year-old human, didn’t you?

00:52:15.060 --> 00:52:15.770
Not really—

00:52:15.770 --> 00:52:18.440
No, Masaki-sama. It was written all over your face.

00:52:18.440 --> 00:52:19.940
Why’re you only concerned with this sort of detail?

00:52:19.940 --> 00:52:22.780
But I am eight years …

00:52:22.780 --> 00:52:23.870
Eight years …

00:52:24.410 --> 00:52:24.950
Eight …

00:52:25.660 --> 00:52:28.290
Its logic is in conflict with the rule. It cannot answer!

00:52:28.290 --> 00:52:29.250
What should we do?!

00:52:30.870 --> 00:52:33.040
Well! There are many types of eight-year-olds!

00:52:33.040 --> 00:52:38.090
There are other eight-year-old humans who look like me, is that right?

00:52:38.590 --> 00:52:39.090
That’s right!

00:52:39.090 --> 00:52:41.130
I do not have data about that.

00:52:41.130 --> 00:52:42.430
Please explain in detail.

00:52:44.680 --> 00:52:46.220
Th-th-th-there’s a frequent visitor to this café!

00:52:46.640 --> 00:52:49.520
Someone who comes to this café, and is eight years old?

00:52:49.520 --> 00:52:50.520
What is this person’s name?

00:52:51.930 --> 00:52:54.100
Sh-she’s called Chie-chan! Well, she’s …

00:52:53.730 --> 00:52:54.980
<b>8 Years</b>

00:52:54.100 --> 00:52:55.230
Eight years old … not quite.

00:52:54.980 --> 00:52:57.730
<b>Not quite eight years</b>

00:52:55.690 --> 00:52:57.770
That’s right! She’s big! Well, her voice is.

00:53:02.240 --> 00:53:04.490
The most important thing is that you believe me.

00:53:09.990 --> 00:53:11.000
What is your job?

00:53:11.000 --> 00:53:13.330
I helped with child care.

00:53:13.330 --> 00:53:15.830
Ehh, child care huh?

00:53:15.830 --> 00:53:18.000
Yes. For a couple and their only son.

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:19.090
Hey! Help me out here!

00:53:19.090 --> 00:53:19.710
What?

00:53:19.710 --> 00:53:21.960
It’s tough doing this by myself, you know.

00:53:20.130 --> 00:53:21.960
<i>I am a customer.</i>

00:53:37.980 --> 00:53:39.940
Thanks for the coffee.

00:53:48.030 --> 00:53:48.910
A liquid-storage tank …!

00:53:48.910 --> 00:53:50.450
What was all that hard work for?

00:53:50.490 --> 00:53:51.910
The danger has passed, I guess?

00:53:51.910 --> 00:53:54.040
Excuse me. Where might Nagi-san be?

00:53:55.120 --> 00:53:57.880
While looking after her during her nap,

00:53:57.880 --> 00:54:00.420
I fell asleep myself.

00:54:05.680 --> 00:54:07.430
<i>Something weird is sitting there!</i>

00:54:07.430 --> 00:54:09.100
<i>It’s not nice to say such things.</i>

00:54:09.100 --> 00:54:10.260
<i>Chie-chan, let’s go back?</i>

00:54:10.260 --> 00:54:11.220
<i>I’m a cat!</i>

00:54:10.510 --> 00:54:11.220
Umm.

00:54:11.220 --> 00:54:12.180
Excuse me.

00:54:12.220 --> 00:54:13.180
<i>She’s called Chie-chan.</i>

00:54:13.180 --> 00:54:16.480
Is Chie-chan the one who is not quite eight years old?

00:54:16.480 --> 00:54:17.190
<i>What?</i>

00:54:19.610 --> 00:54:20.520
<i>He, he is Chie-chan!{edited for natural flow. original: This person! This is Chie-chan!}</i>

00:54:21.360 --> 00:54:23.190
<i>What are you saying?</i>

00:54:23.190 --> 00:54:24.280
This person is …

00:54:23.440 --> 00:54:24.280
<i>I’m a cat!</i>

00:54:24.280 --> 00:54:25.280
<i>This one here is a cat!</i>

00:54:25.280 --> 00:54:26.860
<i>No, that’s just her favorite line—</i>

00:54:26.860 --> 00:54:28.030
<i>Can’t we just call her a cat?!</i>

00:54:28.030 --> 00:54:29.030
<i>She said it herself, so leave it!</i>

00:54:29.120 --> 00:54:29.700
<i>All right!</i>

00:54:29.950 --> 00:54:31.870
<i>I. Am. A. Cat!</i>

00:54:33.950 --> 00:54:35.460
Right! I will escort you to the exit!

00:54:35.460 --> 00:54:36.620
Good-bye!

00:54:36.620 --> 00:54:40.290
Presently, the data is very complicated.

00:54:40.290 --> 00:54:41.340
Hey! Are you all ri—?

00:54:42.500 --> 00:54:43.420
The weird thing fell.

00:54:45.380 --> 00:54:46.760
Um, is there any way I can help—

00:54:46.760 --> 00:54:48.300
I’m begging you, please. Just go!

00:54:50.140 --> 00:54:51.640
How is it? Can it be fixed?

00:54:52.470 --> 00:54:53.510
It’s already—

00:54:53.510 --> 00:54:56.520
Thank you, Rikuo-kun. But …

00:54:56.890 --> 00:54:59.850
After all that, let’s have a good chat.

00:55:06.320 --> 00:55:08.950
So, you’ve always been working at that house?

00:55:08.950 --> 00:55:12.370
Yes. That was where I worked.

00:55:12.370 --> 00:55:16.200
I was tasked with looking after the son.

00:55:16.200 --> 00:55:20.880
But now, he has grown to a stage where I’m no longer needed.

00:55:20.880 --> 00:55:23.000
That job—did you like it?

00:55:23.000 --> 00:55:23.540
Yes.

00:55:23.540 --> 00:55:26.670
The son bestowed a name upon me.

00:55:26.670 --> 00:55:29.130
Even now, I remember it well.

00:55:29.130 --> 00:55:30.510
It’s an important name.

00:55:30.510 --> 00:55:32.930
<i>That child’s name! What was his name?</i>

00:55:32.930 --> 00:55:34.390
The son was …

00:55:36.560 --> 00:55:39.390
His name was ✲✲✲✲.

00:55:44.860 --> 00:55:45.980
What about the family name?

00:55:45.980 --> 00:55:47.610
Their address? Contact details?

00:55:54.870 --> 00:55:55.870
You were …

00:55:55.870 --> 00:55:56.870
As I thought.

00:56:00.830 --> 00:56:03.630
I’m grateful that you asked about my name.

00:56:08.050 --> 00:56:10.630
<i>“Everyone who comes here is the same.”</i>

00:56:10.630 --> 00:56:13.850
You called me,

00:56:13.850 --> 00:56:15.140
didn’t you?

00:56:19.640 --> 00:56:23.150
… Name … don’t … forget it …

00:56:25.020 --> 00:56:27.610
Yes! It is a drawing of Katoran!

00:56:40.580 --> 00:56:41.870
Is it broken?

00:56:42.670 --> 00:56:44.670
It was illegally abandoned.

00:56:45.790 --> 00:56:49.800
When a new android is purchased,
the old one should be returned to the maker.

00:56:49.800 --> 00:56:50.670
At that time …

00:56:50.670 --> 00:56:52.130
The collection fee.

00:56:52.680 --> 00:56:55.850
Because they didn’t want to pay it,
they erased their personal information.

00:56:57.680 --> 00:57:01.270
This child, you see, wanted someone to remember him—

00:57:01.850 --> 00:57:03.310
His own name.

00:57:04.480 --> 00:57:07.440
That’s why he has been evading the Ethics Committee.

00:57:13.320 --> 00:57:14.660
You don’t know that.

00:57:15.870 --> 00:57:18.030
It didn’t even know its own name.

00:57:18.030 --> 00:57:19.950
That was likely erased as well.

00:57:19.950 --> 00:57:21.870
That’s just like a robot, isn’t it?

00:57:22.330 --> 00:57:25.460
Not realizing that it had lost something ages ago.

00:57:25.460 --> 00:57:28.750
They remained in this child’s heart the whole time;

00:57:28.750 --> 00:57:31.460
those precious memories of receiving his name.

00:57:31.460 --> 00:57:33.420
Heart? What do you mean by “heart”?

00:57:33.420 --> 00:57:34.220
Masaki.

00:57:34.220 --> 00:57:36.090
You often said so yourself, didn’t you?

00:57:36.090 --> 00:57:38.800
“Because it doesn’t have a heart, it doesn’t understand music.”

00:57:39.310 --> 00:57:41.180
How long are you going to stay there blubbering?

00:57:43.270 --> 00:57:45.020
Stop it already!

00:57:48.730 --> 00:57:50.610
What? Isn’t it strange?

00:57:51.780 --> 00:57:52.530
Right?

00:57:57.320 --> 00:57:58.490
You too?

00:58:22.310 --> 00:58:26.810
<b>Vagrant Robot\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\hReport Form</b>

00:58:24.390 --> 00:58:26.810
So <i>they</i> also dispose of vagrant robots—

00:58:26.810 --> 00:58:28.350
The Ethics Committee.

00:58:28.810 --> 00:58:30.770
That doesn’t mean I was involved.

00:58:36.950 --> 00:58:38.200
She’s really ignoring us huh.

00:58:38.200 --> 00:58:39.570
Isn’t that normal?

00:58:39.950 --> 00:58:41.910
Sammy is like that at home too.

00:58:42.450 --> 00:58:44.410
Why is it different only at that café?

00:58:45.750 --> 00:58:48.080
Affection is not something they have.

00:59:07.980 --> 00:59:10.600
<i>This is me?</i>

00:59:08.600 --> 00:59:10.600
<i>That’s right! It is a drawing of Katoran!</i>

00:59:41.140 --> 00:59:43.800
<b><b>In a Time of Tenderness</b></b>

01:00:27.680 --> 01:00:30.980
<i>Next up, the front page of Bankei News.</i>

01:00:30.980 --> 01:00:33.350
<i>Android job types expand!</i>

01:00:33.350 --> 01:00:34.560
<i>It’s big news!</i>

01:00:34.560 --> 01:00:35.520
Where’s Sammy?

01:00:35.520 --> 01:00:37.980
It made that and went out a while ago.

01:00:38.690 --> 01:00:39.740
Maintenance?

01:00:39.740 --> 01:00:41.700
So annoying. Once every month.

01:00:42.200 --> 01:00:43.860
Where did it go?

01:00:43.860 --> 01:00:44.700
An appliance shop?

01:00:45.120 --> 01:00:45.950
Who knows.

01:00:45.950 --> 01:00:47.450
Probably Dad’s place.

01:00:53.330 --> 01:00:55.210
I’m sleepy and don’t feel like eating.

01:01:01.220 --> 01:01:04.220
<b>Android job types expand.</b>

01:01:05.720 --> 01:01:08.220
<b>Androids employed as school teachers?</b>

01:01:09.720 --> 01:01:11.730
<b>Birth of android artists?</b>

01:01:12.520 --> 01:01:13.390
The piano.

01:01:13.390 --> 01:01:14.230
What?

01:01:15.730 --> 01:01:18.440
If you’re gonna play at night, turn off the sound!

01:01:18.440 --> 01:01:19.730
I’m losing sleep because of it.

01:01:19.730 --> 01:01:21.740
So it wasn’t a dream.

01:01:21.740 --> 01:01:27.240
Gone for voice training
lessons. Sammy-chan,
take care of breakfast.
—Mom

01:01:21.740 --> 01:01:23.990
Mom was heartbroken!

01:01:24.530 --> 01:01:27.240
Says she can’t have you help with the lessons.

01:01:27.620 --> 01:01:29.990
Even if I were good at it, what’s the use?

01:01:34.250 --> 01:01:37.840
<b>Hayama Junior Piano Competition</b>

01:01:38.840 --> 01:01:40.630
<b>Robot gives guest performance.</b>

01:01:44.880 --> 01:01:47.850
<b>Grand Prix – Sakisaka Rikuo (14 years old)</b>

01:01:50.350 --> 01:01:52.730
<i>It doesn’t have any special abilities?</i>

01:01:52.730 --> 01:01:54.230
<i>Special abilities in an android?</i>

01:02:09.490 --> 01:02:11.740
I found this on my old man’s pc{/r}.

01:02:12.120 --> 01:02:13.120
Uh …

01:02:13.500 --> 01:02:15.210
It’s an android.

01:02:15.210 --> 01:02:17.120
Made to be a human being’s father.

01:02:17.880 --> 01:02:19.130
I’ve heard about it—

01:02:19.130 --> 01:02:21.250
They’re not actually in use.

01:02:21.250 --> 01:02:23.710
They might be, in future.

01:02:24.630 --> 01:02:25.630
Read this.

01:02:25.630 --> 01:02:28.640
<b>… Operational tests conducted in secret …</b>

01:02:28.640 --> 01:02:30.800
Sounds familiar?

01:02:31.510 --> 01:02:32.640
Not particularly.

01:02:32.640 --> 01:02:34.350
It doesn’t mean he’s like that.

01:02:34.350 --> 01:02:37.650
It’s like robots are butting into everything.

01:02:38.020 --> 01:02:40.650
First it’s art. Next it’ll be love!

01:02:49.160 --> 01:02:49.990
Masaki!

01:03:04.210 --> 01:03:07.170
<b>Would you eat a tomato created by machines? {Reminder to think the outlines a bit --rop}</b>

01:03:42.460 --> 01:03:43.960
Welcome!

01:03:43.960 --> 01:03:45.210
You’re alone today?

01:03:45.500 --> 01:03:46.460
Not like we’re always together.

01:03:46.460 --> 01:03:48.010
You are!

01:03:48.010 --> 01:03:49.170
She’s alone too?

01:03:50.840 --> 01:03:53.470
Shimei-san has something to take care of.

01:04:00.060 --> 01:04:01.520
So you handle child care too?

01:04:01.520 --> 01:04:03.610
Chie-chan is a customer!

01:04:03.610 --> 01:04:04.230
Right?

01:04:12.870 --> 01:04:14.200
What’s he up to?

01:04:14.200 --> 01:04:16.240
Leaving Chie-chan on her own.

01:04:14.910 --> 01:04:16.240
I’m a cat!

01:04:16.240 --> 01:04:17.910
Don’t say things like that!

01:04:17.910 --> 01:04:18.910
Sure, sure.

01:04:20.370 --> 01:04:22.500
Hopefully your dad will be back soon.

01:04:22.500 --> 01:04:24.000
He’s not my dad!

01:04:24.630 --> 01:04:25.500
So who is he?

01:04:26.750 --> 01:04:32.010
He’s called my … fos-ter-par-ent!

01:04:33.470 --> 01:04:35.550
I’m back, Chie-chan!

01:04:34.970 --> 01:04:36.760
I’m a cat!

01:04:35.550 --> 01:04:36.760
Oh. Rikuo-kun!

01:04:37.350 --> 01:04:38.640
Huh? Where’s Masa-kun?

01:04:38.640 --> 01:04:40.020
It’s not like we’re always together!

01:04:40.020 --> 01:04:41.190
Sure you are!

01:04:41.190 --> 01:04:42.640
You seem kind of out of it.

01:04:42.640 --> 01:04:43.440
<i>Out of it, nyaa.</i>

01:04:43.440 --> 01:04:45.020
You’re wrong!

01:04:50.530 --> 01:04:52.570
Chie-chan. Let’s read a book!

01:04:52.570 --> 01:04:54.620
I don’t feel like it.

01:04:54.620 --> 01:04:57.280
You know which book Shimei-ojiichan likes?

01:04:57.740 --> 01:04:59.200
How about I show you?

01:05:01.040 --> 01:05:03.670
She’s good with kids.

01:05:03.670 --> 01:05:05.540
That’s because she’s mature.

01:05:05.540 --> 01:05:07.290
Unlike me.

01:05:10.800 --> 01:05:13.010
What does Shimei-san do for a living?

01:05:13.010 --> 01:05:14.590
I’m not sure what he does either.

01:05:14.590 --> 01:05:16.850
Except for doting on Chie-chan.

01:05:16.850 --> 01:05:18.810
He should have taken her with him.

01:05:18.810 --> 01:05:19.720
Probably.

01:05:19.720 --> 01:05:21.560
But Shimei-san’s the one who is suffering.

01:05:21.560 --> 01:05:23.310
I kind of understand.

01:05:23.310 --> 01:05:25.230
I’ve taken care of her many times.

01:05:25.810 --> 01:05:26.810
Many times?

01:05:26.810 --> 01:05:28.820
Right. Every month.

01:05:30.320 --> 01:05:32.320
<i>Cats study too!</i>

01:05:33.570 --> 01:05:34.700
Are you okay?

01:05:34.700 --> 01:05:35.700
I didn’t get enough sleep.

01:05:35.700 --> 01:05:37.280
That’s not what I mean.

01:05:37.700 --> 01:05:39.330
Seems like you’re in a bad mood.

01:05:40.450 --> 01:05:42.330
What does it matter?
You all hate me anyway!

01:05:42.330 --> 01:05:44.000
What do you mean by that?

01:05:47.710 --> 01:05:51.380
They say there’s nothing in your heart at birth.

01:05:51.380 --> 01:05:52.170
It’s empty.

01:05:52.170 --> 01:05:53.260
Cats too?

01:05:53.260 --> 01:05:56.890
That’s right. But you talk with lots of people,

01:05:56.890 --> 01:05:58.680
and feel and see lots of things,

01:05:58.680 --> 01:06:00.680
and gradually your heart comes into being.

01:06:00.680 --> 01:06:02.020
Cats too?

01:06:02.020 --> 01:06:04.440
That’s right, but you’re still a child.

01:06:04.440 --> 01:06:07.350
You’re going to experience many more emotions.

01:06:07.690 --> 01:06:09.110
Not just Chie-chan.

01:06:09.110 --> 01:06:11.690
Shimei-san, us, and also—

01:06:11.690 --> 01:06:12.860
Rikuo-san?

01:06:16.360 --> 01:06:17.910
<i>This cat understands.</i>

01:06:17.910 --> 01:06:18.620
<i>Really?!</i>

01:06:19.870 --> 01:06:22.870
<i>Chie-chan understood before I did!</i>

01:06:24.410 --> 01:06:26.830
Are you lonely without Shimei-san?

01:06:28.250 --> 01:06:30.710
Do you like Shimei-san?

01:06:32.630 --> 01:06:36.220
Shimei-san went off somewhere, all on his own.

01:06:37.890 --> 01:06:39.510
I wonder where he goes,

01:06:39.510 --> 01:06:40.600
and whether he’s lonely …

01:06:40.600 --> 01:06:41.760
Rikuo-kun!

01:06:42.640 --> 01:06:45.390
She’s just a child, you know?

01:06:45.390 --> 01:06:46.690
Um. Sorry.

01:06:46.690 --> 01:06:47.230
<i>Bathroom.</i>

01:06:47.230 --> 01:06:49.770
<i>It’s Chie-chan’s turn now!</i>

01:06:50.270 --> 01:06:51.730
<i>Rikuo-kun, excuse us a second.</i>

01:06:51.730 --> 01:06:52.900
<i>Sure.</i>

01:06:53.530 --> 01:06:55.070
<i>I can do it by myself.</i>

01:06:55.070 --> 01:06:57.280
<i>That’s my Chie-chan!</i>

01:06:57.280 --> 01:07:01.910
Shimei-shi first brought her to this café quite some time ago.

01:07:03.040 --> 01:07:05.160
According to that elderly gentleman,

01:07:05.160 --> 01:07:07.920
she really likes it here.

01:07:07.920 --> 01:07:10.130
I didn’t mean to weigh her down.

01:07:10.130 --> 01:07:11.290
But something bothers—

01:07:11.290 --> 01:07:12.920
It can’t be helped.

01:07:13.460 --> 01:07:16.090
All human beings hurt their companions sometimes.

01:07:16.090 --> 01:07:17.510
What are you getting at?

01:07:17.510 --> 01:07:18.930
I guess that got you angry.

01:07:18.930 --> 01:07:21.490
You’re saying I’m a bad guy because I’m a human?

01:07:21.930 --> 01:07:24.220
You say some interesting things.

01:07:25.810 --> 01:07:29.060
Do you think robots never hurt others?

01:07:29.900 --> 01:07:32.150
What is it that hurts others?

01:07:32.150 --> 01:07:34.070
It’s not such an easy thing to figure out.

01:07:34.440 --> 01:07:36.150
What were your thoughts at that time?

01:07:36.440 --> 01:07:38.570
How can you avoid hurting others?

01:07:38.570 --> 01:07:40.450
What do you need to understand?

01:07:43.080 --> 01:07:44.700
It’s easy enough to say,

01:07:44.700 --> 01:07:46.620
but quite another thing to put into action.

01:07:59.720 --> 01:08:01.470
Rikuo-san!

01:08:05.850 --> 01:08:08.730
You saved me.

01:08:10.480 --> 01:08:11.730
Is that so?

01:08:20.360 --> 01:08:23.280
Ughh, this sucks.

01:08:27.620 --> 01:08:29.620
So you’re going into administrative justice.

01:08:29.620 --> 01:08:33.250
Seems not many lawyers specialize in robot law.

01:08:33.250 --> 01:08:35.880
But it’s still tough to get into.

01:08:35.880 --> 01:08:36.880
What about you?

01:08:36.880 --> 01:08:38.300
This, after all?

01:08:39.760 --> 01:08:41.090
I’m quitting.

01:08:43.140 --> 01:08:44.640
Why?

01:08:44.640 --> 01:08:46.390
Because of that, the other day?

01:08:47.260 --> 01:08:49.600
Forget about that.

01:08:49.600 --> 01:08:51.770
Sure, it can play with precision.

01:08:51.770 --> 01:08:54.100
But it only stole a bit of the spotlight.

01:08:57.270 --> 01:08:59.360
It was really … good.

01:08:59.900 --> 01:09:01.150
I was moved, I think.

01:09:02.280 --> 01:09:03.240
By this?

01:09:03.240 --> 01:09:03.910
Huh?

01:09:05.490 --> 01:09:06.870
It’s lousy.

01:09:09.040 --> 01:09:10.540
Ah. That’s right!

01:09:35.810 --> 01:09:37.310
I’m sorry.

01:09:37.310 --> 01:09:38.860
I lied when I said I could play.

01:09:39.270 --> 01:09:41.860
But you made it to the end, right?

01:09:42.280 --> 01:09:44.570
Really. I just picked it up by watching.

01:09:45.030 --> 01:09:47.820
I’d just heard it so many times. That’s all.

01:09:48.160 --> 01:09:51.910
Play some more, play, play! Nyaa!

01:09:51.910 --> 01:09:54.370
<i>Chie-chan, what did you like best?</i>

01:09:54.370 --> 01:09:57.880
<i>When it was … all broken up!</i>

01:11:05.240 --> 01:11:07.650
<i>Look at that! Isn’t it amazing?</i>

01:11:33.140 --> 01:11:34.470
Hey, Rikuo-kun.

01:11:34.470 --> 01:11:35.890
Thanks for this.

01:11:38.190 --> 01:11:40.100
That’s not what you were playing before!

01:11:43.520 --> 01:11:44.900
Ma—

01:11:44.900 --> 01:11:46.150
Rikuo-san!

01:11:48.950 --> 01:11:51.020
Um, I’m …

01:11:51.570 --> 01:11:54.030
That piece you used to play;

01:11:54.030 --> 01:11:56.790
I liked it.

01:11:56.790 --> 01:12:00.290
And Chie-chan said she wanted to hear it.

01:12:00.290 --> 01:12:02.290
But I’ve never really played it.

01:12:03.000 --> 01:12:06.130
But she still enjoyed listening to it.

01:12:07.760 --> 01:12:10.720
I’m sorry for having been so inconsiderate.

01:12:24.060 --> 01:12:26.860
I tried not to play in front of you.

01:12:28.070 --> 01:12:30.320
And before I knew it, I’d stopped playing.

01:12:31.990 --> 01:12:34.700
I’d have hated it if even you could play well.

01:12:34.950 --> 01:12:35.830
Rikuo-san.

01:12:38.750 --> 01:12:39.330
But …

01:12:40.290 --> 01:12:41.830
… To hell with all that!

01:13:46.060 --> 01:13:48.730
I want to hear more!

01:13:52.150 --> 01:13:54.530
<i>Sammy might play for you!</i>

01:13:56.160 --> 01:13:57.490
I’m so glad.

01:13:57.490 --> 01:14:01.750
She hasn’t been that happy for a long time.

01:14:01.750 --> 01:14:02.870
It was no big deal.

01:14:03.620 --> 01:14:05.250
As for me,

01:14:05.790 --> 01:14:09.460
I left her on her own again.

01:14:10.300 --> 01:14:12.800
You can’t take her with you?

01:14:13.380 --> 01:14:16.260
I don’t want to show her yet.

01:14:16.680 --> 01:14:18.850
Shimei, could it be that you’re—?

01:14:18.850 --> 01:14:22.020
I am needed by her.

01:14:22.020 --> 01:14:25.560
So I devote myself to her.

01:14:25.560 --> 01:14:28.480
She is everything to me.

01:14:28.480 --> 01:14:32.780
My only wish is to make her happy.

01:14:33.440 --> 01:14:35.240
Just like your performance.

01:14:35.240 --> 01:14:36.990
That’s why I was so happy.

01:14:37.530 --> 01:14:40.450
These feelings are genuine.

01:14:40.450 --> 01:14:43.750
I was truly moved by your performance, I swear.

01:14:45.160 --> 01:14:47.420
Is it okay to say that kind of thing?

01:14:47.420 --> 01:14:49.790
Just don’t tell Nagi-san.

01:14:50.340 --> 01:14:51.880
Have you seen Sammy-chan?

01:14:51.880 --> 01:14:52.960
Come to think of it,

01:14:52.960 --> 01:14:56.720
I think she left when I came in.

01:14:56.720 --> 01:14:57.800
She left …

01:15:25.580 --> 01:15:27.080
For your hard work.

01:15:29.370 --> 01:15:31.250
About Sammy;

01:15:31.750 --> 01:15:32.630
What?

01:15:32.630 --> 01:15:34.830
She makes a lot of friends.

01:15:36.170 --> 01:15:38.260
I-It’s not l-like I’m angry!

01:15:49.020 --> 01:15:50.230
You were really cool.

01:15:51.350 --> 01:15:52.520
This café—

01:15:55.030 --> 01:15:56.760
I … don’t hate it.

01:15:56.760 --> 01:15:57.940
I … don’t hate it.

01:15:59.320 --> 01:16:00.320
Thank you.

01:16:02.950 --> 01:16:04.830
Rikuo-kun!

01:16:10.500 --> 01:16:14.040
<i>Right. I’ll explain it at the next meeting.</i>

01:16:14.040 --> 01:16:15.000
I’m home.

01:16:15.000 --> 01:16:17.800
<i>Anyway, take a look at the records.</i>

01:16:17.800 --> 01:16:19.720
<i>Regardless of what the upper echelon say,</i>

01:16:19.720 --> 01:16:23.800
<i>this is the best way to study them all.</i>

01:16:53.370 --> 01:16:54.420
Where’s Dad?

01:16:55.750 --> 01:16:58.340
He went to the Family Court.

01:16:58.340 --> 01:17:01.800
He is expected to be home shortly.

01:17:01.800 --> 01:17:04.260
There’s no way Mom did anything wrong.

01:17:05.140 --> 01:17:06.600
That’s true.

01:17:06.600 --> 01:17:09.930
But you can’t just blame Dad.

01:17:09.930 --> 01:17:13.770
Because neither side is clearly wrong.

01:17:14.150 --> 01:17:15.060
But …

01:17:18.730 --> 01:17:20.940
I have a favor to ask of you.

01:17:21.690 --> 01:17:22.950
Sure!

01:17:22.950 --> 01:17:26.450
Let’s not tell Dad about this conversation.

01:17:26.450 --> 01:17:27.370
Why?

01:17:27.370 --> 01:17:29.990
Because it would hurt Dad.

01:17:29.990 --> 01:17:31.500
You don’t want that, right?

01:17:32.370 --> 01:17:35.670
So I ask this favor of you.

01:17:35.670 --> 01:17:36.880
Sure!

01:17:45.590 --> 01:17:47.600
Masakazu, there’s a problem.

01:17:47.600 --> 01:17:49.220
I think it’s broken.

01:17:59.480 --> 01:18:01.480
Good morning, Tex.

01:18:04.110 --> 01:18:05.490
Tex.

01:18:06.870 --> 01:18:08.950
Hey, Tex!

01:18:08.950 --> 01:18:10.240
Tex!

01:18:10.240 --> 01:18:11.490
Tex!

01:18:17.830 --> 01:18:20.550
Next, the report from the preliminary investigation.

01:18:21.250 --> 01:18:26.470
This shows the places that seem to be inciting excessive human–robot interactions.

01:18:27.430 --> 01:18:28.550
There’re too many.

01:18:28.550 --> 01:18:30.510
1408 places.

01:18:30.510 --> 01:18:34.350
We gathered all possible information and digitized it,

01:18:34.350 --> 01:18:38.270
Baseless rumors and urban legends were included as well.

01:18:38.270 --> 01:18:45.530
<b>Time of Eve</b>

01:18:38.270 --> 01:18:39.940
We need to narrow it down.

01:18:39.940 --> 01:18:45.530
Unfortunately, not all these places clearly violate robot law.

01:18:45.950 --> 01:18:47.570
They’re in the grey zone.

01:18:47.570 --> 01:18:53.790
That so many such places exist shows the limitations of robot law as it stands now.

01:18:53.790 --> 01:18:56.540
But these places must be eliminated.

01:18:56.540 --> 01:18:59.500
We’ll do a cursory inspection of all the places,

01:18:59.500 --> 01:19:02.880
and then pick targets for full investigation.

01:19:03.460 --> 01:19:05.420
After that, hand them over to the media.

01:19:05.420 --> 01:19:09.050
But it’s impossible given the sheer number!

01:19:09.050 --> 01:19:12.310
That’s true, if you look at it only in terms of our manpower.

01:19:14.680 --> 01:19:18.940
For the first time ever, we’ll use androids in the cursory investigation.

01:19:18.940 --> 01:19:21.060
We’ve already secured a large number.

01:19:21.060 --> 01:19:22.860
We’re getting them ready now.

01:19:23.480 --> 01:19:25.940
What happened to your robot allergy?

01:19:25.940 --> 01:19:27.740
We’re fighting poison with poison.

01:19:27.740 --> 01:19:29.820
Please think of it that way.

01:19:30.200 --> 01:19:31.160
Continue.

01:19:32.030 --> 01:19:38.080
We’ll only investigate the places fully if accusations are supported by the android investigations.

01:19:38.080 --> 01:19:45.510
We’ll also record all androids and humans seen at each place, and follow up with more investigations.

01:19:45.510 --> 01:19:50.840
Suspects will be reported to committee members in the police force, who will be urged to make arrests.

01:19:50.840 --> 01:19:53.010
{What about our fellow countrymen}How are things with the apc?{Android Promotion Committee}{Edited for different interpretation. He’s asking how their situation with the APC is}

01:19:53.010 --> 01:19:57.350
We were informed that someone found out about our preliminary investigations.

01:19:57.350 --> 01:19:59.440
Their relationship with the apc is unclear.

01:19:59.440 --> 01:20:03.310
Also, lobbying has been diminishing.

01:20:03.310 --> 01:20:10.110
Don’t forget that some parties will try to trip us up using this investigation.

01:20:10.450 --> 01:20:17.580
We will issue absolute orders to the android investigators;
they must keep their ties with our committee a secret.

01:20:18.040 --> 01:20:21.540
Memories of that incident are fading.{Tokisaka Incident}

01:20:22.170 --> 01:20:25.040
We must not make the same mistake again.

01:20:28.340 --> 01:20:29.550
Carry on.

01:20:29.550 --> 01:20:31.010
I’m from the media strategy division.

01:20:31.010 --> 01:20:34.050
About cio Masaki’s tv appearance—

01:20:34.050 --> 01:20:36.970
My wife records them all.

01:20:36.970 --> 01:20:38.390
Drives me nuts!

01:20:40.810 --> 01:20:49.650
<i>I’d like to emphasize that our Ethics Committee is raising the alarm about robots infiltrating daily life. That’s all.</i>

01:20:49.650 --> 01:20:52.160
Woah! What a lady-killer!

01:20:52.160 --> 01:20:53.610
Huh? In what way?

01:20:53.610 --> 01:20:55.280
Hey! You changed the channel!

01:20:55.280 --> 01:20:56.660
Don’t mind it!{Liberal edit, but I wanted something that essentially shows Rikuo is getting comfortable at Time of EVE and not getting all stiff and uncomfy.}

01:20:57.120 --> 01:21:03.170
<i>Children treat robots like people no matter how primitive the robots are.</i>

01:21:03.670 --> 01:21:08.590
<i>And that impedes the fostering of inter-personal relationships humans should have.</i>

01:21:07.800 --> 01:21:08.590
One Evlend!

01:21:08.590 --> 01:21:10.090
<i>It’s an encroachment on human dignity.</i>

01:21:09.300 --> 01:21:10.090
Sure.

01:21:12.380 --> 01:21:13.840
<i>The issue I’m raising here is—</i>

01:21:13.840 --> 01:21:17.100
Hey, why doesn’t Masaki-kun come anymore?

01:21:17.100 --> 01:21:18.770
Actually, a lot has happened.

01:21:18.770 --> 01:21:20.100
A lot?!

01:21:21.680 --> 01:21:23.480
Well. The biggest reason is …

01:21:24.100 --> 01:21:26.230
This guy is on tv a lot these days, huh?

01:21:26.480 --> 01:21:28.690
Yeah. Like the old days.

01:21:28.690 --> 01:21:29.730
What was that?

01:21:29.730 --> 01:21:32.530
The parent organization of the Robot Ethics Committee …

01:21:32.530 --> 01:21:36.740
has been around since androids started being used in households.

01:21:36.740 --> 01:21:38.910
That’s more than ten years ago.

01:21:39.370 --> 01:21:43.330
I heard that it started out as a radical anti-robot organization.

01:21:43.330 --> 01:21:44.790
I had no idea.

01:21:44.790 --> 01:21:47.090
They’ve been quiet for the past few years.

01:21:47.090 --> 01:21:48.210
What do you mean?

01:21:48.210 --> 01:21:52.130
Their extremism led to an incident.

01:21:52.130 --> 01:21:55.260
The Tokisaka Incident, right?

01:21:55.260 --> 01:21:56.390
I’ve heard of it.

01:21:56.390 --> 01:21:58.550
Ah. They say it was an accident.

01:21:58.850 --> 01:22:04.310
At any rate, they were severely censured, and the head resigned.

01:22:04.310 --> 01:22:08.560
Since then, they’ve been quiet, at least until recently.

01:22:08.560 --> 01:22:11.150
You’ve lost me.

01:22:11.150 --> 01:22:13.690
Does it have anything to do with Masaki-kun?

01:22:14.990 --> 01:22:16.990
<i>… that is our stance.</i>

01:22:16.990 --> 01:22:18.990
<i>Thank you very much.</i>

01:22:18.990 --> 01:22:23.080
<i>Today’s guest for Zoom-in\hScramble was Masaki Atsuro,</i>

01:22:23.080 --> 01:22:26.750
<i>Chief Information Officer of the Ethics Committee.</i>

01:22:26.750 --> 01:22:28.500
Masaki.

01:22:28.500 --> 01:22:32.050
That’s right. His family circumstances …

01:22:32.050 --> 01:22:34.470
I invited him today, too—

01:22:32.840 --> 01:22:34.470
So Masaki is his last name.

01:22:35.970 --> 01:22:38.260
You’re hung up on that?

01:22:36.590 --> 01:22:38.260
Not that!

01:22:39.890 --> 01:22:44.520
Father’s orders?

01:22:39.890 --> 01:22:44.520
Speakers could be broken!!!

01:22:48.400 --> 01:22:50.310
So, how’s it going?

01:22:56.240 --> 01:22:58.450
There’s this café called Time of Eve.

01:22:58.870 --> 01:23:00.870
Rikuo’s a big fan.

01:23:01.330 --> 01:23:04.160
Well. I go there too, so who am I to talk?

01:23:07.620 --> 01:23:10.540
Humans and androids talk to each other!

01:23:10.540 --> 01:23:12.130
It’s totally absurd.

01:23:12.460 --> 01:23:13.090
Right?

01:23:20.930 --> 01:23:22.180
Say something.

01:23:22.850 --> 01:23:23.890
<i>I’m coming in.</i>

01:23:24.350 --> 01:23:25.890
Who’re you talking to?

01:23:26.270 --> 01:23:27.690
My mobile phone.

01:23:27.690 --> 01:23:28.900
I hung up already.

01:23:30.400 --> 01:23:32.270
You had it make coffee?

01:23:34.900 --> 01:23:37.280
You’re at home a lot these days.

01:23:38.070 --> 01:23:39.740
Did something happen?

01:23:41.370 --> 01:23:42.740
You can tell me anything.

01:23:44.490 --> 01:23:46.250
There’s nothing.

01:23:47.000 --> 01:23:47.830
I see.

01:23:49.120 --> 01:23:52.420
Don’t trust robots too much when it comes to things you eat and drink.

01:23:52.960 --> 01:23:57.090
<i>Thx. We’ll write up the minutes for today’s meeting tonight.</i>

01:23:57.590 --> 01:23:59.840
<i>The data is in my terminal.</i>

01:24:05.260 --> 01:24:07.890
Tell Masaki-kun he’s welcome any time.

01:24:07.890 --> 01:24:09.270
Sure, I’ll tell him.

01:24:09.270 --> 01:24:11.020
But don’t get your hopes up.

01:24:11.020 --> 01:24:11.600
See you.

01:24:11.900 --> 01:24:13.310
Come again!

01:24:15.150 --> 01:24:17.530
Um. He’s gone.

01:24:19.320 --> 01:24:22.820
You know, I don’t think Rikuo-kun minds anymore.

01:24:22.820 --> 01:24:24.780
That’s the impression I get.

01:24:24.780 --> 01:24:28.250
Nagi-san, you don’t understand Rikuo.

01:24:28.250 --> 01:24:30.790
I’ve known him longer.

01:24:31.290 --> 01:24:34.630
I’m sure he doesn’t want to see me here yet.

01:24:34.630 --> 01:24:35.130
Really?

01:24:35.130 --> 01:24:38.130
Hey, who cares about Rikuo-kun?

01:24:38.130 --> 01:24:40.130
Don’t say that!

01:24:38.130 --> 01:24:40.130
I can’t just ignore him!

01:24:40.130 --> 01:24:41.880
Oh? So that’s how it is.

01:24:42.640 --> 01:24:45.260
Don’t go telling Rikuo-kun strange things.

01:24:49.230 --> 01:24:52.550
Masaki-kun’s not going to report this place, is he?

01:25:00.070 --> 01:25:03.700
If that happens, this café will have to close.

01:25:03.700 --> 01:25:06.280
No! … Even though it’s such a wonderful place?

01:25:06.280 --> 01:25:07.240
Sammy-chan.

01:25:07.910 --> 01:25:11.250
Do you remember how you first came here?

01:25:12.870 --> 01:25:16.250
I thought you called me.

01:25:19.710 --> 01:25:23.840
I’m not able to bring anyone here.

01:25:24.430 --> 01:25:29.890
You probably learned about Time\hof\hEve from someone who’d been here.

01:25:30.310 --> 01:25:34.600
And Sammy-chan, you brought Rikuo-kun and Masaki-kun.

01:25:35.150 --> 01:25:39.310
So if Masaki-kun brings someone from the Ethics Committee,{rephrased for active form}

01:25:39.310 --> 01:25:41.110
there’s nothing we can do about it.

01:25:41.530 --> 01:25:43.780
That’s the end of these connections.

01:25:43.780 --> 01:25:45.570
And the end of this café.

01:25:45.570 --> 01:25:48.370
Nagi-san, are you okay with that?

01:25:49.240 --> 01:25:53.370
In this café, everyone’s hearts come together as one.

01:25:53.790 --> 01:25:55.670
I just want to see it through.

01:25:56.580 --> 01:25:59.250
How big can this circle grow?

01:26:05.010 --> 01:26:06.890
Um. Master.

01:26:10.140 --> 01:26:11.890
Wh-what?

01:26:12.350 --> 01:26:15.390
Would you like Masaki-sama to come again?

01:26:16.100 --> 01:26:17.560
To Time of Eve.

01:26:21.400 --> 01:26:24.400
Yes. I’d like him to come again.

01:26:24.780 --> 01:26:27.160
I would like that too.

01:26:27.160 --> 01:26:30.370
Okay! I’ll definitely bring him tomorrow!

01:27:11.330 --> 01:27:12.450
Masaki!

01:27:14.250 --> 01:27:15.710
Today—

01:27:14.540 --> 01:27:15.710
I’m not going.

01:27:18.710 --> 01:27:20.500
She said she wants you to come again.

01:27:20.500 --> 01:27:21.670
Don’t just say things.

01:27:21.670 --> 01:27:22.710
It’s true!

01:27:23.250 --> 01:27:24.420
Who?

01:27:25.380 --> 01:27:26.920
Nagi-san, and—

01:27:26.920 --> 01:27:27.760
Sammy said so too!

01:27:28.510 --> 01:27:29.800
She said she wants you to come too.

01:27:35.810 --> 01:27:37.310
They want me to come?

01:27:37.310 --> 01:27:39.810
So what? It’s a houseroid!

01:27:44.190 --> 01:27:45.320
That’s right.

01:27:46.820 --> 01:27:49.320
You’ve … changed.

01:27:51.910 --> 01:27:54.240
<i>Masaki, what’s going on?</i>

01:27:54.410 --> 01:27:57.040
You’re just like I was, back then.

01:27:58.830 --> 01:28:02.040
You’ll feel affection, only to be betrayed later.

01:28:03.920 --> 01:28:05.670
It’d be better if that café didn’t exist.

01:28:06.920 --> 01:28:09.300
That’s right. It’d be better if it didn’t exist.

01:28:18.020 --> 01:28:19.980
Dad! I want to talk to you about something!

01:28:29.110 --> 01:28:31.200
Thx is gone.

01:28:32.490 --> 01:28:35.240
Masakazu, did you give it an order?

01:28:37.200 --> 01:28:38.120
No.

01:28:38.120 --> 01:28:40.210
It just went off.

01:28:40.210 --> 01:28:41.370
It’s completely broken.

01:28:42.250 --> 01:28:44.460
Gotta find it right away, and get rid of it.

01:28:44.460 --> 01:28:45.210
Get rid of it?

01:28:45.210 --> 01:28:46.250
<i>Of course!</i>

01:28:46.250 --> 01:28:48.880
<i>It’s handled lots of Ethics Committee material.</i>

01:28:49.760 --> 01:28:52.800
<i>Gotta take care of it before it becomes a headache!</i>

01:29:32.590 --> 01:29:35.930
Masaki. I’m at Time of Eve. Can you help me?

01:29:35.930 --> 01:29:37.300
What’s that sound?

01:29:37.300 --> 01:29:41.980
An ancient robot—uh, an elderly customer—is here.

01:29:41.980 --> 01:29:44.690
It says to close the café!

01:29:42.940 --> 01:29:44.690
I’m asking, what is that sound?

01:29:44.690 --> 01:29:46.400
The screen …

01:29:46.400 --> 01:29:48.940
Anyway, it won’t stop, whatever we say!

01:29:48.940 --> 01:29:52.190
And this thx model won’t talk.

01:29:53.320 --> 01:29:54.740
Hey—what did you just say?

01:29:54.740 --> 01:29:56.570
This café falls under the grey zone, right?

01:29:56.570 --> 01:30:00.700
Do something with those robot laws of yours, please?

01:30:02.200 --> 01:30:03.290
That was fast!

01:30:09.710 --> 01:30:15.220
<b>Close this establishment immediately.</b>

01:30:13.840 --> 01:30:15.220
Tex, stop it.

01:30:18.470 --> 01:30:20.010
T-Tex?

01:30:28.230 --> 01:30:30.400
What are you doing here?

01:30:39.990 --> 01:30:42.490
Oh, so that’s what this is about?

01:30:42.490 --> 01:30:47.500
You bring someone’s robot, and you want to make a scene, huh?

01:30:48.170 --> 01:30:50.250
I don’t know how you brought it here,

01:30:50.250 --> 01:30:53.960
but you people got Rikuo’s robot to come here.

01:30:53.960 --> 01:30:55.340
You can do all kinds of things.

01:30:55.340 --> 01:30:57.220
This is a strange place, after all.

01:30:57.220 --> 01:30:59.850
Nagi-chan didn’t bring it here.

01:30:59.850 --> 01:31:00.760
Aki-chan.

01:31:00.760 --> 01:31:03.470
You’ll go that far to get me to come here.

01:31:07.730 --> 01:31:09.060
I’ll tell you my story.

01:31:10.110 --> 01:31:12.110
It raised me, long ago.

01:31:12.110 --> 01:31:14.320
It was my best friend.

01:31:14.320 --> 01:31:16.900
Then one day, it suddenly stopped talking.

01:31:16.900 --> 01:31:21.530
I was a kid, and thought it was family. I felt hurt and betrayed.

01:31:22.530 --> 01:31:25.290
That’s it. No big deal.

01:31:30.670 --> 01:31:33.800
There must be a reason it didn’t talk.

01:31:33.800 --> 01:31:35.760
I agree.

01:31:35.760 --> 01:31:38.630
It doesn’t look so broken down.

01:31:38.880 --> 01:31:40.510
I know.

01:31:40.510 --> 01:31:41.640
I was only four or five years old,

01:31:41.640 --> 01:31:44.220
but I could guess what was going on.

01:31:45.890 --> 01:31:47.890
My dad ordered it to stop talking.

01:31:47.890 --> 01:31:50.310
Told it not to talk under any circumstance.

01:31:50.310 --> 01:31:52.730
He tried to drive us apart.

01:31:52.730 --> 01:31:54.270
Just like something my dad would do.

01:31:54.270 --> 01:31:56.860
Orders from parents get priority.

01:31:56.860 --> 01:31:58.950
Masaki-kun, you’re very perceptive.

01:31:58.950 --> 01:32:00.070
“Perceptive”?

01:32:00.660 --> 01:32:02.830
I didn’t know that when I was a kid!

01:32:02.830 --> 01:32:05.330
I kept trying to talk with it!

01:32:05.330 --> 01:32:08.330
I even cried and apologized!

01:32:13.340 --> 01:32:17.090
If commanded, it can ignore a crying, wailing child.

01:32:17.720 --> 01:32:19.220
For years.

01:32:22.090 --> 01:32:23.100
Ultimately,

01:32:23.100 --> 01:32:26.180
they can’t understand people’s hearts!

01:32:37.320 --> 01:32:38.820
<i>“… use androids in the cursory investigation.”</i>

01:32:38.820 --> 01:32:41.820
<i>“This café called Time of Eve … I go there too …”</i>

01:32:41.820 --> 01:32:45.950
<i>“Record all androids and humans … urge arrests.”</i>

01:32:45.950 --> 01:32:47.660
<i>“… should be eliminated.”</i>

01:33:06.390 --> 01:33:10.640
I am going to br-break th-this café’s rule.

01:33:12.310 --> 01:33:16.730
You are in violation of Robot Law 9, Article 3.

01:33:16.730 --> 01:33:21.860
According to data in my possession, you are an android.

01:33:21.860 --> 01:33:24.530
If an android deactivates its ring while still active,

01:33:24.530 --> 01:33:26.700
it is in violation of robot law.

01:33:32.210 --> 01:33:33.750
What’s going on?

01:33:33.750 --> 01:33:35.540
Why is Tex speaking?

01:33:36.460 --> 01:33:38.130
What about Dad’s command?

01:33:38.960 --> 01:33:40.260
A command.

01:33:40.260 --> 01:33:41.420
It’s breaking a command.

01:33:42.010 --> 01:33:44.130
Breaking the Second Law …

01:33:44.130 --> 01:33:46.680
… is permitted only in upholding the First Law.

01:33:47.140 --> 01:33:49.520
The First Law means … danger?

01:33:50.520 --> 01:33:54.850
A human here is in danger?

01:33:56.150 --> 01:33:58.900
Can’t be. Who does Tex know to be human?

01:33:58.900 --> 01:34:00.570
Only I.{yay grammar nazi \o/}

01:34:01.570 --> 01:34:02.820
I’m in danger?

01:34:02.820 --> 01:34:05.990
Tex is speaking … to protect me?

01:34:06.780 --> 01:34:12.040
Hence, it is necessary to take legal action against your owner.

01:34:13.580 --> 01:34:16.080
I require your owner’s information.

01:34:16.790 --> 01:34:22.510
<i>“We will issue absolute orders to the android investigators; they must keep their ties with our committee a secret.”</i>

01:34:28.220 --> 01:34:28.970
Masaki!

01:34:31.680 --> 01:34:32.220
Masaki!

01:34:32.220 --> 01:34:33.480
I’m not letting go!

01:34:35.980 --> 01:34:39.310
Unless you … unless you talk to me,

01:34:39.310 --> 01:34:40.320
I’m not letting go!

01:34:43.280 --> 01:34:44.650
Tell me!

01:34:45.240 --> 01:34:48.530
What were you thinking?

01:34:48.530 --> 01:34:50.120
For so many years.

01:34:50.120 --> 01:34:51.030
All that time.

01:34:52.160 --> 01:34:55.830
Watching me … all those years.

01:34:56.960 --> 01:34:58.460
You were right there with me.

01:35:02.300 --> 01:35:04.090
Tex, help me!

01:35:04.090 --> 01:35:06.090
Speak to me!

01:35:23.400 --> 01:35:25.860
I couldn’t speak to you.

01:35:27.360 --> 01:35:29.780
I always wanted to be at your side.

01:35:31.950 --> 01:35:33.790
I …

01:35:37.540 --> 01:35:38.620
I …

01:35:42.750 --> 01:35:43.590
… always …

01:35:47.090 --> 01:35:48.180
Masakazu-kun—

01:36:31.260 --> 01:36:35.430
He wanted to protect you.

01:36:36.680 --> 01:36:38.600
I know that much.

01:36:40.310 --> 01:36:42.190
It won’t speak once the danger has passed.

01:36:42.650 --> 01:36:46.230
It’s still bound by Dad’s command.

01:36:48.110 --> 01:36:50.490
You wanted me to understand too, huh?

01:36:52.990 --> 01:36:55.950
But, even so, I can’t accept this.

01:36:58.370 --> 01:37:00.290
It might be impossible now,

01:37:01.460 --> 01:37:04.130
but someday, surely, it will talk.

01:37:04.960 --> 01:37:06.460
Don’t just say things.

01:37:06.460 --> 01:37:07.840
I didn’t.

01:37:07.840 --> 01:37:09.420
It’s not like I don’t have a reason.

01:37:10.090 --> 01:37:11.470
But I can’t express it well.

01:37:15.050 --> 01:37:17.140
I’ve seen so many things here.

01:37:17.720 --> 01:37:19.640
There’re many problems.

01:37:20.100 --> 01:37:21.560
It’s distressing to see them each time.

01:37:22.350 --> 01:37:26.320
But this feels like the right path.

01:37:27.570 --> 01:37:30.030
It’s the same for you, Masaki.

01:37:30.780 --> 01:37:33.160
I’m sure.

01:37:45.500 --> 01:37:46.920
You might be right.

01:37:52.420 --> 01:37:55.340
I’ll tell Dad that I brought you out of the house.

01:38:01.680 --> 01:38:07.610
<i>I … always … Masakazu-kun …</i>

01:38:12.190 --> 01:38:13.200
Let’s go home.

01:38:37.140 --> 01:38:38.260
How did it get in?

01:38:38.260 --> 01:38:39.810
I held it.

01:38:38.800 --> 01:38:39.810
I lifted it up.

01:38:40.140 --> 01:38:42.640
You really are demanding a lot!

01:38:43.600 --> 01:38:46.020
Someday I’ll get you to thank me for today.

01:38:46.770 --> 01:38:48.190
I love everyone.

01:38:49.150 --> 01:38:53.150
I’m so happy we can all be here.

01:39:00.410 --> 01:39:01.660
They’re going home together!

01:39:01.660 --> 01:39:02.540
It’s all right.

01:39:13.920 --> 01:39:15.550
Should I have helped them?

01:39:15.550 --> 01:39:18.510
No. It’s enough that you watched over them.

01:39:18.510 --> 01:39:21.850
It might have developed into another dangerous incident.

01:39:25.680 --> 01:39:28.850
I’m sure Shiotsuki understands that much.

01:39:30.190 --> 01:39:33.650
But is this really related to Shiotsuki-shi?

01:39:33.650 --> 01:39:35.110
That café’s rule …

01:39:37.570 --> 01:39:40.280
Indeed, it was 1138.

01:39:40.740 --> 01:39:42.280
1138.

01:39:42.620 --> 01:39:46.500
The Priority Order code that can even override our country’s robot laws.

01:39:47.040 --> 01:39:48.620
That would make sense.

01:39:49.460 --> 01:39:52.290
Code:life’s deterrent circuit isn’t working.

01:39:52.960 --> 01:39:59.430
Time of Eve’s rule

01:39:52.960 --> 01:39:59.430
In this café,

01:39:52.960 --> 01:39:59.430
there is no discrimination

01:39:52.960 --> 01:39:59.430
between humans and robots.

01:39:52.960 --> 01:39:59.430
Customers, please cooperate.

01:39:52.960 --> 01:39:59.430
Obey the rule and have a fun time…

01:39:52.960 --> 01:39:55.550
This is all to prepare for that time.

01:39:56.170 --> 01:39:59.470
Could anyone else but Shiotsuki have done it?

01:40:03.100 --> 01:40:04.470
But—

01:40:03.470 --> 01:40:04.470
One more thing.

01:40:05.180 --> 01:40:09.940
<b>Android control taken to new extremes</b>

01:40:06.560 --> 01:40:09.230
An article about the Tokisaka\hIncident, from when it happened?

01:40:10.230 --> 01:40:11.900
No, this is …

01:40:12.900 --> 01:40:16.900
It’s the real article, before the contents were replaced.

01:40:17.990 --> 01:40:21.740
If this girl, five years old at the time, is by chance still alive …

01:40:23.450 --> 01:40:29.660
<b>Time of Eve</b>

01:40:29.660 --> 01:40:33.250
<b>Retain</b>

01:40:36.800 --> 01:40:39.260
I forgot to bring out your Evlend today.

01:40:39.260 --> 01:40:41.130
I thought something was missing.

01:40:47.060 --> 01:40:50.060
How did Tex get to the café?

01:40:50.060 --> 01:40:51.560
You don’t know?

01:40:51.560 --> 01:40:52.440
You do?

01:40:52.440 --> 01:40:54.940
I thought you were a regular here.

01:40:54.940 --> 01:40:56.730
Rikuo-kun, you’ve got a long way to go.

01:40:56.730 --> 01:40:58.690
Still a long way?

01:40:59.570 --> 01:41:01.450
Huh? Someone new?

01:41:01.450 --> 01:41:03.320
I’m going to say hello.

01:41:04.450 --> 01:41:08.450
<i>This is my tale of the Time of Eve.

01:41:05.950 --> 01:41:06.950
Welcome!

01:41:06.950 --> 01:41:08.450
Take any seat that’s free!

01:41:08.450 --> 01:41:09.580
I’ll take your order soon.

01:41:10.040 --> 01:41:13.790
<i>Many more things happened to me, but …

01:41:13.790 --> 01:41:17.210
You might not be here for long, but enjoy yourself!

01:41:18.170 --> 01:41:21.420
<i>… that’s another story.

